-- | Ipe.Db.Store — typed, injection-safe persistence over the audited `Ipe.Db`.
--
-- A `Store a` is one classified, queryable table whose row type is `a`,
-- described once by a `Codec a`. The codec is the single source of truth: its
-- `Shape` yields the table's columns and their types, its encoder writes a row,
-- and its decoder reads one back. Identifiers are declared once, validated once,
-- and reused by every read and write. The safe surface cannot express SQL
-- injection: every table and column identifier reaches SQL only after
-- `validSqlIdent` accepts it, and every value binds as a parameter, never
-- interpolated. A caller who wants raw SQL must reach for `Ipe.Db.Unsafe`, which
-- discloses that capability program-wide.
--
-- Deny-by-default access, made structural: a table is not queryable until its
-- access intent is declared. `fromCodec` returns a `Draft a` — a table whose
-- schema is known but whose access intent is not. A `Draft a` has no read or
-- write operation; the schema-shaping builders (`primaryKey` / `serial` /
-- `unique` / `defaultNow` / `index` / …) refine it, but nothing reads or writes
-- it. Classification promotes a `Draft a` to a queryable value, and it is the
-- only path to one:
--
--   * `public : Draft a -> Store a` — an explicit, greppable, auditable
--     declaration that the table is world-readable and world-writable. The reads
--     and writes (`all` / `get` / `findWhere` / `insert` / `insertReturning` /
--     `update` / …) accept a `Store a`.
--   * `secured : Policy a -> Draft a -> Result Error (Secured a)` — attaches a
--     row-security `Policy` and yields a `Secured a`, read and written only
--     through the authenticated `…As` operations (`allAs` / `getAs` / …), each
--     of which takes a `Principal`.
--
-- No read or write function accepts a `Draft a`, and a `Store a` / `Secured a`
-- exists only via `public` / `secured`, so a table someone forgot to classify is
-- unqueryable by construction (make-invalid-states-unrepresentable). `public` is
-- a first-class term, so "which tables are world-open" is a code search for
-- `Store.public`, not an audit of every `fromCodec` site.
--
-- Minimal flow:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store, Draft)
--     import Ipe.Codec as Codec exposing (Codec)
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--
--     type alias Country =
--         { code : String, name : String }
--
--     -- A world-readable reference table: an explicit `public` declaration.
--     countries : Result Error (Store Country)
--     countries =
--         case Store.fromCodec "countries" countryCodec of
--             Err e ->
--                 Err e
--
--             Ok draft ->
--                 Ok (Store.public (Store.primaryKey .code draft))
--
--     -- A per-owner table: `secured` attaches the policy, yielding a `Secured`.
--     documents : Result Error (Store.Secured Document)
--     documents =
--         case Store.fromCodec "documents" documentCodec of
--             Err e ->
--                 Err e
--
--             Ok draft ->
--                 Store.secured (Store.ownerColumn .owner)
--                     (Store.serial .id draft)
--
-- The security argument, made structural:
--
--   * Identifiers are parsed, not validated. A `Store` is built through
--     `fromCodec` (or the raw-column escape `fromColumns`), which runs
--     `validSqlIdent` over the table name and every derived column name and
--     returns `Err` on the first non-conforming identifier. A built `Store`
--     therefore carries only accepted identifiers — no downstream operation
--     re-checks, because an invalid one was unrepresentable past the constructor
--     (parse-don't-validate).
--   * Columns derive from the codec, never drift from it. `fromCodec` reads the
--     codec's `Shape`; a non-`SRecord` codec names no columns and is a build
--     `Err` (fail-closed). The column list, the DDL, the insert binds, and the
--     row decode are one derivation from one codec, so a field added or retyped
--     changes exactly one place.
--   * Values never touch SQL text. `insert` projects the row through the codec
--     to typed `SqlField` binds (`Ipe.Db.Codec.codecToBinds`) and routes them
--     through `Db.insertFields`; `all` / `get` / `findWhere` route through
--     `Db.findWhere` over a `SqlFragment` built from `Sql.column` (validated) +
--     `Sql.param` (bound) and decode each row through the codec
--     (`Ipe.Db.Codec.codecFromRow`); `delete` routes through `Db.deleteWhere`
--     the same way. Not one of them concatenates a caller value into SQL.
--   * Reads decode through the codec, so schema drift (a missing column, a cell
--     that is not the declared shape) is a typed `Err`, never a wrong value.
--   * The by-key operations use the store's own validated primary key, never a
--     caller-supplied identifier string.
--   * `create` is fail-closed. It builds the `CREATE TABLE` DDL from the store's
--     typed columns, re-validating every identifier through `validSqlIdent`
--     before it can appear in the DDL string, and hands the result to the
--     versioned `Db.migrate` ledger (idempotent, additive). A store whose columns
--     do not all validate produces `Err` and issues no SQL.
--   * Schema-evolution operators (`renameColumn`, `renameTable`) are total
--     constructors that record ops in an ordered log. `migrations` is the single
--     point where rename DDL text is assembled: every identifier (table name,
--     rename `from`, rename `to`) passes through `validSqlIdent` before it can
--     reach any SQL string, the first failure returns `Err` and produces no SQL,
--     and no value is ever interpolated. The rename surface is non-destructive:
--     only `ALTER … RENAME` is generated; `DROP COLUMN` and similar data-losing
--     alters are absent. Drift between the CRUD column identifiers and the
--     migration column identifiers is unrepresentable because both derive from
--     the same `Store` value.
--
-- Write-path safety: columns marked `Serial`, `DefaultNow`, or `touchOnUpdate`
-- are OMITTED from every client write so the database fills them (the existing
-- `SqlField`/`OmitField` seam) — a client value produced for such a column is
-- dropped, never written.
--
-- Building and using a codec-driven store:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Codec as Codec exposing (Codec)
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     -- A `Codec User` whose `Shape` is `SRecord [("id", CText), …]` (built with
--     -- the `Ipe.Codec` record combinators or `Codec.auto`).
--     userStore : Result Error (Store User)
--     userStore =
--         Store.fromCodec "users" userCodec
--             |> Result.map (Store.primaryKey .id)
--             |> Result.map Store.public
--
-- The column-spec builders (`primaryKey`, `serial`, …) name their column by an
-- accessor literal (`.id`), checked against the row type at compile time, and
-- shape the `Draft` before classification. A raw-column escape (below) has no row
-- type, so its columns are named by a bare string through the capability-gated
-- `Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe`.
--
-- The raw-column escape (a dynamic or non-codec table) builds a `Store Row`
-- whose rows are the untyped `Dict String String` the DB layer returns; read a
-- column back with the typed `read*` helpers:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe as StoreU
--
--     rawStore : Result Error (Store Store.Row)
--     rawStore =
--         Store.fromColumns "users"
--             [ Store.textColumn "id"
--             , Store.textColumn "name"
--             , Store.intColumn "age"
--             ]
--             |> Result.map (StoreU.primaryKey "id")
--             |> Result.map Store.public
--
--     readUser : Store.Row -> Result Error User
--     readUser row =
--         Result.map3 (\i n a -> { id = i, name = n, age = a })
--             (Store.readText "id" row)
--             (Store.readText "name" row)
--             (Store.readInt "age" row)
--
-- A misdeclared identifier — a table or column name with a quote, a semicolon,
-- or a space — makes the constructor return `Err`, so it never reaches SQL.
--
-- Joining two stores (one parameterized statement, no per-row fan-out):
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     -- `join` names the two join columns by accessor (`.authorId` = `.id`);
--     -- `joinFilterRight` restricts the right side; `joinToList` returns each
--     -- matched pair, both full rows decoded through their own store codec.
--     activeAuthorBooks : Db -> Store Book -> Store Author -> Task Error (List ( Book, Author ))
--     activeAuthorBooks db books authors =
--         Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--             |> Store.joinFilterRight (Store.eq .active True)
--             |> Store.joinToList db
--
-- lowers to one statement:
--
--     select a0.id as a0__id, a0.title as a0__title, a0.author_id as a0__author_id,
--            a1.id as a1__id, a1.name as a1__name, a1.active as a1__active
--     from books as a0, authors as a1
--     where (a0.author_id = a1.id) and (a1.active = ?)
--
-- Every table, alias, and column is a validated identifier; the filter value is
-- a bound parameter, never interpolated. `joinFilterLeft` filters the left side,
-- `joinToMaybe` / `joinCount` read the first pair / the match count.
module Ipe.Db.Store exposing
    ( Draft(..)
    , Store(..)
    , Column(..)
    , ColType(..)
    , ColumnSpec(..)
    , IndexSpec(..)
    , Row
    , validSqlIdent
    , column, textColumn, intColumn, floatColumn, boolColumn
    , fromCodec, fromColumns, rowCodec
    , public
    , primaryKey, serial, unique, defaultNow
    , defaultText, defaultInt, touchOnUpdate
    , primaryKeyNamed, serialNamed, uniqueNamed, defaultNowNamed
    , defaultTextNamed, defaultIntNamed, touchOnUpdateNamed
    , index, indexNamed
    , tableName, columnNames, createSql
    , readText, readInt, readBool, readFloat
    , insert, insertReturning, all, get, delete, findWhere, create
    , allOn, getOn, findWhereOn
    , update, updateWhere, deleteWhere, findBy
    , renameColumn, renameTable, migrations
    , noopRenameError
    , Cond(..), CompareOp(..), Query
    , query, where
    , eq, eqBy
    , neq, neqBy
    , gt, gtBy
    , gte, gteBy
    , lt, ltBy
    , lte, lteBy
    , like
    , isNull, notNull
    , inList, inListBy
    , and, or, not
    , orderAsc, orderDesc, limit, offset
    , toList, toMaybe, count
    , Order(..)
    , Joined
    , join, joinNamed
    , joinFilterLeft, joinFilterRight
    , orderByLeft, orderByRight
    , orderByLeftNamed, orderByRightNamed
    , joinToList, joinToMaybe, joinCount
    , Select
    , select, selectNamed, literal, upper, lower, coalesce
    , selectToList, selectToMaybe
    , Policy, Secured
    , unrestricted, publicRead, ownerColumn, immutable, andPolicy, secured
    , ownerColumnNamed, immutableNamed
    , allAs, getAs, insertAs, updateAs, deleteAs
    )


import Ipe.Auth as Auth
import Ipe.Basics as Basics
import Ipe.Codec as Codec exposing (Codec(..), ColType(..), Shape(..))
import Ipe.Db as Db
import Ipe.Db.Codec as DbCodec
import Ipe.Db.Dsn as Dsn exposing (Connection)
import Ipe.Db.Sql as Sql
import Ipe.Ffi as Ffi
import Ipe.Json.Encode as Encode
import Ipe.Config as Config
import Ipe.Char as Char
import Ipe.String as String
import Ipe.List as List
import Ipe.Dict as Dict
import Ipe.Maybe as Maybe exposing (Maybe(..))
import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
import Ipe.Task as Task
import Ipe.Error as Error exposing (Error)


-- ─── Types ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | A row read back from the database: every column keyed by its name, values
-- as strings (the `Db.findWhere` representation). The typed `read*` helpers
-- parse a column back into its Ipê type; the raw-column escape (`fromColumns`)
-- stores rows in this form.
type alias Row =
    Dict String String


-- A store's columns are typed with `Ipe.Codec.ColType` — the dialect-neutral
-- column type a codec also derives — so one codec's shape and a hand-declared
-- store share a single column vocabulary. The `Ipe.Db` kernels map each
-- `ColType` to the configured backend's concrete SQL type, so one store
-- declaration works on every backend.


-- | One typed column: its (validated) name and its abstract type for the
-- schema. The value projection lives in the store's codec, so a `Column` is
-- pure data — no stored function.
type Column
    = Column
        { name : String
        , colType : ColType
        }


-- | A DB-only fact the record type cannot express: primary key, serial
-- (DB-assigned) id, uniqueness, or a DB-stamped default. A typed ADT, not a
-- stringly flag — an impossible spec (a flag typo, a malformed default) is
-- unrepresentable.
type ColumnSpec
    = PrimaryKey String
    | Serial String
    | Unique String
    | DefaultNow String
    | DefaultText String String
    | DefaultInt String Int
    | TouchOnUpdate String


-- | A declarative performance index over one or more of the store's columns.
-- Declared through `index` (name derived from table + columns) or `indexNamed`
-- (caller-supplied name). The columns are validated against the store's
-- codec-derived column list at `create` time; an unknown column or an invalid
-- identifier fails closed with no DDL produced.
type IndexSpec
    = Index (List String)
    | IndexNamed String (List String)


-- | A schema-evolution operation recorded in the store's op log. Each op is
-- appended by `renameColumn` / `renameTable`; the log folds to ledger entries
-- in `migrations`. Kept as a closed ADT so `migrations` is the single point
-- that builds DDL text from ops — identifiers are validated there, never here.
type SchemaOp
    = RenameColumn String String
    | RenameTable String String


-- | A table under construction: its schema is known but its access intent is
-- not yet declared, so it is NOT queryable. `fromCodec` / `fromColumns` return a
-- `Draft a`, the schema-shaping builders (`primaryKey` / `serial` / `unique` /
-- `defaultNow` / `defaultText` / `defaultInt` / `touchOnUpdate` / `index` /
-- `indexNamed`) refine one, and `public` / `secured` are the only ways to promote
-- one to a queryable `Store a` / `Secured a`. No read or write operation accepts
-- a `Draft a`, so a table left unclassified cannot be read or written by mistake
-- (deny-by-default).
--
-- The record fields are the same schema payload a `Store a` carries: `codec` is
-- the single source of truth (its `Shape` yields the columns and their types),
-- `specs` are the DB-only facts, `pk` is the validated primary-key column,
-- `frozenTable` / `frozenColumns` hold the table name and columns as first
-- constructed, `table` / `currentColumns` track the current-schema view after any
-- renames, `ops` is the ordered schema-op log, and `indexes` is the ordered index
-- specs. Built only through `fromCodec` / `fromColumns`, so every identifier it
-- holds has already been accepted by `validSqlIdent`.
type Draft a
    = Draft
        { table : String
        , frozenTable : String
        , frozenColumns : List Column
        , currentColumns : List Column
        , codec : Codec a
        , specs : List ColumnSpec
        , pk : Maybe String
        , ops : List SchemaOp
        , indexes : List IndexSpec
        }


-- | One classified, queryable table whose schema, reads, and writes derive from
-- its `Codec a`. A `Store a` is reachable only through `public` (an explicit,
-- auditable world-open declaration) applied to a `Draft a`, so every queryable
-- store has passed through a deliberate classification. Its record fields carry
-- the same schema payload the `Draft a` held: `codec` is the single source of
-- truth (its `Shape` yields the columns and their types, its encoder writes a
-- row, its decoder reads one back); `specs` are the DB-only facts; `pk` is the
-- validated primary-key column used by the by-key operations. `frozenTable` and
-- `frozenColumns` hold the table name and columns as first constructed (the
-- source for the never-drifting create entry — neither is edited by a rename).
-- `table` and `currentColumns` track the current-schema view after any renames
-- (the identifiers CRUD targets). `ops` is the ordered schema-op log folded by
-- `migrations`, threaded from `frozenTable` so each rename entry names the table
-- as of its own position. `indexes` is the ordered list of declarative index
-- specs emitted after the create entry by `create` / `migrations`.
type Store a
    = Store
        { table : String
        , frozenTable : String
        , frozenColumns : List Column
        , currentColumns : List Column
        , codec : Codec a
        , specs : List ColumnSpec
        , pk : Maybe String
        , ops : List SchemaOp
        , indexes : List IndexSpec
        }


-- ─── Identifier validation (parse-don't-validate) ──────────────────


-- | `validSqlIdent name` — accept `name` as an SQL identifier, or reject it.
-- The ONLY gate through which a table or column name reaches emitted SQL on the
-- safe surface. Accepts a non-empty run of ASCII letters, digits, `_`, and `.`
-- (a dotted `table.column` reference); rejects everything else — a space, a
-- quote, a semicolon, a parenthesis. This mirrors the identifier gate the
-- `Ipe.Db` kernels apply, so the Ipê-level check and the runtime check agree:
-- an identifier that passes here also passes the kernel, and one that fails
-- here is rejected before any SQL is built.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.validSqlIdent "users" == True
--     Store.validSqlIdent "users.id" == True
--     Store.validSqlIdent "user table" == False
--     Store.validSqlIdent "users;drop" == False
validSqlIdent : String -> Bool
validSqlIdent name =
    Basics.not (String.isEmpty name) && String.all validIdentChar name


validIdentChar : Char -> Bool
validIdentChar c =
    let
        code =
            Char.toCode c
    in
    isAsciiDigit code
        || isAsciiUpper code
        || isAsciiLower code
        || code == underscoreCode
        || code == dotCode


isAsciiDigit : Int -> Bool
isAsciiDigit code =
    code >= 48 && code <= 57


isAsciiUpper : Int -> Bool
isAsciiUpper code =
    code >= 65 && code <= 90


isAsciiLower : Int -> Bool
isAsciiLower code =
    code >= 97 && code <= 122


underscoreCode : Int
underscoreCode =
    95


dotCode : Int
dotCode =
    46


-- ─── Column builders ───────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `column name colType` — a column of an explicit `ColType`. The name is
-- validated when the store is built, not here, so a raw `Column` is inert until
-- a constructor accepts it.
column : String -> ColType -> Column
column name colType =
    Column { name = name, colType = colType }


-- | A `CText` column.
textColumn : String -> Column
textColumn name =
    column name CText


-- | A `CInt` column.
intColumn : String -> Column
intColumn name =
    column name CInt


-- | A `CReal` column.
floatColumn : String -> Column
floatColumn name =
    column name CReal


-- | A `CBool` column.
boolColumn : String -> Column
boolColumn name =
    column name CBool


-- ─── Store construction (fail-closed) ──────────────────────────────


-- | `fromCodec table codec` — build a `Draft` from a record codec, or fail
-- closed. The columns are derived from the codec's `Shape`: an `SRecord`
-- becomes the typed column list, a non-`SRecord` codec (a bare scalar, a blob)
-- names no columns and is an `Err`. The table name and every derived column
-- name are run through `validSqlIdent`; the first non-conforming identifier is
-- an `Err`, and no draft is produced. A returned `Draft` therefore holds only
-- accepted identifiers, and its columns cannot drift from the codec that reads
-- and writes its rows (parse-don't-validate + one source of truth).
--
-- A `Draft` is NOT queryable: classify it with `public` (world-open) or
-- `secured` (policy-guarded) to obtain a value the read and write operations
-- accept (deny-by-default).
fromCodec : String -> Codec a -> Result Error (Draft a)
fromCodec table codec =
    case Codec.shape codec of
        SRecord cols ->
            buildStore table (List.map columnFromShape cols) codec

        SScalar _ ->
            Err notARecordError

        SBlob ->
            Err notARecordError


columnFromShape : ( String, ColType ) -> Column
columnFromShape pair =
    case pair of
        ( name, colType ) ->
            column name colType


-- | `fromColumns table columns` — the raw-column escape: build a `Draft Row`
-- for a dynamic or non-codec table, whose rows are the untyped
-- `Dict String String` the DB layer returns. The draft's codec is the row codec
-- derived from `columns` (`rowCodec`), so the same codec-driven CRUD applies once
-- the draft is classified: writes bind each declared column, reads decode back
-- into a `Row`, and the typed `read*` helpers parse a column into its Ipê type.
-- Like `fromCodec`, the table name and every column name run through
-- `validSqlIdent`, so an invalid identifier fails closed before any SQL is built.
-- Classify the result with `public` / `secured` before querying (deny-by-default).
fromColumns : String -> List Column -> Result Error (Draft Row)
fromColumns table columns =
    buildStore table columns (rowCodec columns)


-- The shared constructor for both `fromCodec` and `fromColumns`: validate the
-- table name and every column name, then freeze the columns and attach the
-- codec, producing an unclassified `Draft`. The single validation boundary
-- (parse-don't-validate).
buildStore : String -> List Column -> Codec a -> Result Error (Draft a)
buildStore table columns codec =
    if Basics.not (validSqlIdent table) then
        Err (invalidIdentError "table" table)

    else
        case firstInvalidColumn columns of
            Just bad ->
                Err (invalidIdentError "column" bad)

            Nothing ->
                Ok
                    (Draft
                        { table = table
                        , frozenTable = table
                        , frozenColumns = columns
                        , currentColumns = columns
                        , codec = codec
                        , specs = []
                        , pk = Nothing
                        , ops = []
                        , indexes = []
                        }
                    )


firstInvalidColumn : List Column -> Maybe String
firstInvalidColumn columns =
    case columns of
        [] ->
            Nothing

        first :: rest ->
            let
                name =
                    columnName first
            in
            if validSqlIdent name then
                firstInvalidColumn rest

            else
                Just name


columnName : Column -> String
columnName col =
    case col of
        Column r ->
            r.name


columnColType : Column -> ColType
columnColType col =
    case col of
        Column r ->
            r.colType


-- ─── Row codec (the raw-column escape's source of truth) ───────────


-- | `rowCodec columns` — the `Codec Row` a raw-column store uses: a record
-- codec over `Dict String String` whose `Shape` is `SRecord` naming each
-- declared column. Every column is carried as `CText` at the JSON layer,
-- because a `Row` cell is already the string form the DB layer returns and
-- accepts (the same lossless-string basis the `read*` helpers parse from); the
-- DDL still uses each column's declared `ColType` (held on the store's frozen
-- columns), so a raw store creates its table with real types while its rows stay
-- untyped strings. The encoder emits the declared columns as a JSON object; the
-- decoder reads that object back into a `Row`.
rowCodec : List Column -> Codec Row
rowCodec columns =
    let
        names =
            List.map columnName columns
    in
    Codec
        { enc = \row -> Encode.object (rowFields names row)
        , mkDec = \_ -> Config.dict Config.string
        , shp = SRecord (List.map (\name -> ( name, CText )) names)
        }


-- | Project a `Row` to the JSON fields for the declared columns, in order. A
-- column absent from the row emits the empty string, so the encoded object
-- always names every declared column (the shape's contract), never a missing
-- field the bind step would reject.
rowFields : List String -> Row -> List ( String, Value )
rowFields names row =
    List.map (\name -> ( name, Encode.string (rowCell name row) )) names


rowCell : String -> Row -> String
rowCell name row =
    case Dict.get name row of
        Just s ->
            s

        Nothing ->
            ""


-- ─── Classification (the only Draft → queryable promotions) ─────────


-- | `public draft` — classify `draft` as a world-readable, world-writable table
-- and return the queryable `Store a`. This is the explicit, auditable escape
-- hatch: a code search for `Store.public` enumerates exactly the tables a
-- reviewer must confirm are meant to be open, because `public` is a first-class
-- declaration, not the absence of one. It is the only promotion from a `Draft` to
-- a `Store`; `secured` is the policy-guarded alternative (deny-by-default: an
-- unclassified `Draft` is unqueryable by construction).
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--
--     countries : Result Error (Store Country)
--     countries =
--         case Store.fromCodec "countries" countryCodec of
--             Err e ->
--                 Err e
--
--             Ok draft ->
--                 Ok (Store.public (Store.primaryKey .code draft))
public : Draft a -> Store a
public draft =
    case draft of
        Draft r ->
            Store
                { table = r.table
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = r.specs
                , pk = r.pk
                , ops = r.ops
                , indexes = r.indexes
                }


-- ─── Column-spec builders ──────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Each attaches one typed `ColumnSpec` to the `Draft`, before classification,
-- because it shapes the schema, not the access policy. `primaryKey` additionally
-- records the validated key column the by-key operations read. The identifier
-- was validated when the draft was built, so no spec can smuggle an unchecked
-- name into SQL.


-- | Mark the accessor-named column the primary key. The lowering intercept
-- extracts the column name from the accessor and calls `primaryKeyNamed`.
primaryKey : (row -> t) -> Draft row -> Draft row
primaryKey =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_primaryKey"


-- | Mark the accessor-named column DB-assigned (serial). `insert` omits it so
-- the database generates it — a client value can never overwrite a serial column.
serial : (row -> t) -> Draft row -> Draft row
serial =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_serial"


-- | Mark the accessor-named column unique.
unique : (row -> t) -> Draft row -> Draft row
unique =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_unique"


-- | Mark the accessor-named column DB-stamped with the current time on insert.
-- `insert` omits it so the database stamps it.
defaultNow : (row -> t) -> Draft row -> Draft row
defaultNow =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_defaultNow"


-- | `defaultText accessor value store` — give the accessor-named column a
-- DB-level `DEFAULT` of the text `value`. The literal is emitted through the
-- same single-quote-doubling escape SQL string literals require, so no caller
-- text escapes the literal — a `value` containing a quote cannot break out into SQL.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--
--     withStatusDefault : Store { status : String } -> Store { status : String }
--     withStatusDefault store =
--         Store.defaultText .status "active" store
defaultText : (row -> String) -> String -> Draft row -> Draft row
defaultText =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_defaultText"


-- | `defaultInt accessor value store` — give the accessor-named column a
-- DB-level `DEFAULT` of the integer `value`. An integer has no injection
-- surface: it renders as its own decimal digits, so the emitted `DEFAULT`
-- literal is always safe.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--
--     withRetryDefault : Store { retries : Int } -> Store { retries : Int }
--     withRetryDefault store =
--         Store.defaultInt .retries 0 store
defaultInt : (row -> Int) -> Int -> Draft row -> Draft row
defaultInt =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_defaultInt"


-- | Mark the accessor-named column a DB-stamped updated-at column. Like
-- `defaultNow` it gets `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` in the schema and is OMITTED
-- from every client write (`insert`, `update`, `updateWhere`), so a client value
-- can never overwrite it — the database owns the column's value.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--
--     withUpdatedAt : Store { updatedAt : String } -> Store { updatedAt : String }
--     withUpdatedAt store =
--         Store.touchOnUpdate .updatedAt store
touchOnUpdate : (row -> t) -> Draft row -> Draft row
touchOnUpdate =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_touchOnUpdate"


-- The stringly forms below are reached by the accessor-typed kernel intercepts
-- above, and are exported for the capability-gated `Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe` string
-- surface (a raw-column `fromColumns` draft has no row type, so its columns can
-- only be named by a bare string). They shape the `Draft`, before classification.

primaryKeyNamed : String -> Draft a -> Draft a
primaryKeyNamed name draft =
    case draft of
        Draft r ->
            Draft
                { table = r.table
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = PrimaryKey name :: r.specs
                , pk = Just name
                , ops = r.ops
                , indexes = r.indexes
                }


serialNamed : String -> Draft a -> Draft a
serialNamed name draft =
    addSpec (Serial name) draft


uniqueNamed : String -> Draft a -> Draft a
uniqueNamed name draft =
    addSpec (Unique name) draft


defaultNowNamed : String -> Draft a -> Draft a
defaultNowNamed name draft =
    addSpec (DefaultNow name) draft


defaultTextNamed : String -> String -> Draft a -> Draft a
defaultTextNamed name value draft =
    addSpec (DefaultText name value) draft


defaultIntNamed : String -> Int -> Draft a -> Draft a
defaultIntNamed name value draft =
    addSpec (DefaultInt name value) draft


touchOnUpdateNamed : String -> Draft a -> Draft a
touchOnUpdateNamed name draft =
    addSpec (TouchOnUpdate name) draft


addSpec : ColumnSpec -> Draft a -> Draft a
addSpec spec draft =
    case draft of
        Draft r ->
            Draft
                { table = r.table
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = spec :: r.specs
                , pk = r.pk
                , ops = r.ops
                , indexes = r.indexes
                }


-- ─── Declarative index builders ────────────────────────────────────


-- | `index columns draft` — declare a non-unique performance index over one or
-- more columns of `draft`, with the index name derived from the table name and
-- the column list (`idx_<table>_<col1>_<col2>_…`). Every column in `columns`
-- must be a name present in the draft's codec-derived column list; an unknown
-- column or an invalid identifier fails closed at `create` time. Shaping the
-- schema, this refines the `Draft` before classification.
--
-- The index is emitted as a separate `CREATE INDEX` ledger entry after the
-- `CREATE TABLE` entry in `migrations`. Existing indexes are not affected
-- (idempotent when the ledger already records this entry name).
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--
--     type alias Post =
--         { id : Int, authorId : String, publishedAt : String }
--
--     postsStore : Result Error (Store Post)
--     postsStore =
--         Store.fromCodec "posts" postCodec
--             |> Result.map (Store.serial .id)
--             |> Result.map (Store.primaryKey .id)
--             |> Result.map (Store.index [ "author_id" ])
--             |> Result.map (Store.index [ "author_id", "published_at" ])
--             |> Result.map Store.public
index : List String -> Draft a -> Draft a
index columns draft =
    addIndex (Index columns) draft


-- | `indexNamed name columns draft` — declare a non-unique performance index
-- over one or more columns of `draft`, with a caller-supplied index name.
-- `name` must pass `validSqlIdent`; every column in `columns` must be a name
-- present in the draft's codec-derived column list. An invalid name, an unknown
-- column, or an invalid column identifier fails closed at `create` time. Shaping
-- the schema, this refines the `Draft` before classification.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--
--     type alias Post =
--         { id : Int, authorId : String, publishedAt : String }
--
--     postsStore : Result Error (Store Post)
--     postsStore =
--         Store.fromCodec "posts" postCodec
--             |> Result.map (Store.serial .id)
--             |> Result.map (Store.primaryKey .id)
--             |> Result.map (Store.indexNamed "posts_author_published" [ "author_id", "published_at" ])
--             |> Result.map Store.public
indexNamed : String -> List String -> Draft a -> Draft a
indexNamed name columns draft =
    addIndex (IndexNamed name columns) draft


addIndex : IndexSpec -> Draft a -> Draft a
addIndex spec draft =
    case draft of
        Draft r ->
            Draft
                { table = r.table
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = r.specs
                , pk = r.pk
                , ops = r.ops
                , indexes = r.indexes ++ [ spec ]
                }


-- ─── Schema-evolution operators ────────────────────────────────────


-- | `renameColumn from to store` — record a column rename in the store's
-- schema-op log and update the current-column view so subsequent
-- `insert` / `get` / `all` target `to`. The frozen create columns are
-- unchanged; the rename appears as a separate ledger entry when `migrations`
-- is called.
--
-- This function is a total constructor: `from` and `to` are admitted into the
-- op log unconditionally. Identifier validation happens once, in `migrations`,
-- which is the only point SQL text is built — an invalid name therefore
-- surfaces as `Err` there, not here.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe as StoreU
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--     import Ipe.Db as Db
--     import Ipe.Task as Task
--
--     -- Build a store with a column rename in the schema-op log, then
--     -- render it to a migration list and apply it.
--     applyRename : Db -> Task Error (List String)
--     applyRename conn =
--         let
--             storeResult =
--                 Store.fromColumns "users"
--                     [ Store.textColumn "id"
--                     , Store.textColumn "name"
--                     , Store.intColumn "age"
--                     ]
--                     |> Result.map (StoreU.primaryKey "id")
--                     |> Result.map Store.public
--                     |> Result.map (Store.renameColumn "name" "full_name")
--         in
--         case storeResult |> Result.andThen Store.migrations of
--             Ok ms ->
--                 Db.migrate conn ms
--
--             Err e ->
--                 Task.fail e
renameColumn : String -> String -> Store a -> Store a
renameColumn from to store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Store
                { table = r.table
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = renameColumnInList from to r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = r.specs
                , pk = r.pk
                , ops = r.ops ++ [ RenameColumn from to ]
                , indexes = r.indexes
                }


renameColumnInList : String -> String -> List Column -> List Column
renameColumnInList from to cols =
    List.map
        (\col ->
            if columnName col == from then
                column to (columnColType col)

            else
                col
        )
        cols


-- | `renameTable to store` — record a table rename in the store's schema-op
-- log and update the store's active table name so subsequent `insert` / `get`
-- / `all` target `to`. The frozen create DDL retains the original table name;
-- the rename appears as a separate ledger entry when `migrations` is called.
--
-- Like `renameColumn`, this is a total constructor: `to` is admitted
-- unconditionally and validated in `migrations` when SQL text is built.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe as StoreU
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--     import Ipe.Db as Db
--     import Ipe.Task as Task
--
--     -- Build a store with a table rename, render to migrations, and apply.
--     applyTableRename : Db -> Task Error (List String)
--     applyTableRename conn =
--         let
--             storeResult =
--                 Store.fromColumns "users"
--                     [ Store.textColumn "id"
--                     , Store.textColumn "email"
--                     ]
--                     |> Result.map (StoreU.primaryKey "id")
--                     |> Result.map Store.public
--                     |> Result.map (Store.renameTable "accounts")
--         in
--         case storeResult |> Result.andThen Store.migrations of
--             Ok ms ->
--                 Db.migrate conn ms
--
--             Err e ->
--                 Task.fail e
renameTable : String -> Store a -> Store a
renameTable to store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Store
                { table = to
                , frozenTable = r.frozenTable
                , frozenColumns = r.frozenColumns
                , currentColumns = r.currentColumns
                , codec = r.codec
                , specs = r.specs
                , pk = r.pk
                , ops = r.ops ++ [ RenameTable r.table to ]
                , indexes = r.indexes
                }


-- | `migrations store` — fold the store's schema-op log to the ordered list of
-- ledger entries `Db.migrate` consumes: the frozen create entry (built from the
-- store's columns as first constructed) followed by one `ALTER … RENAME` entry
-- per rename op.
--
-- This is the single point where rename DDL text is assembled. Every identifier
-- that will appear in SQL — the table name and each rename's `from`/`to` — is
-- checked through `validSqlIdent` before it reaches the string. The first
-- failing identifier returns `Err` and produces no SQL. A no-op rename
-- (`from == to`) is also rejected with `noopRenameError`.
--
-- For a store with no rename ops, `migrations` yields exactly the frozen create
-- entry — identical to calling `Store.create`, which routes through here.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store.Unsafe as StoreU
--     import Ipe.Result as Result exposing (Result(..))
--     import Ipe.Db as Db
--     import Ipe.Task as Task
--
--     -- Render a store (with a column rename) to its ordered migration list.
--     buildMigrations : Db -> Task Error (List String)
--     buildMigrations conn =
--         let
--             storeResult =
--                 Store.fromColumns "users"
--                     [ Store.textColumn "id"
--                     , Store.textColumn "name"
--                     , Store.intColumn "age"
--                     ]
--                     |> Result.map (StoreU.primaryKey "id")
--                     |> Result.map Store.public
--                     |> Result.map (Store.renameColumn "name" "full_name")
--         in
--         case storeResult |> Result.andThen Store.migrations of
--             Ok ms ->
--                 -- ms = [ { name = "create_users", sql = "CREATE TABLE …" }
--                 --       , { name = "rename_column_users_name_to_full_name"
--                 --         , sql = "ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name"
--                 --         }
--                 --       ]
--                 Db.migrate conn ms
--
--             Err e ->
--                 Task.fail e
migrations : Store a -> Result Error (List Db.Migration)
migrations store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case createSqlFromColumns r.frozenTable r.frozenColumns r.specs of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok ddl ->
                    let
                        createEntry =
                            { name = migrationName r.frozenTable, sql = ddl }
                    in
                    case foldOps r.frozenTable r.ops of
                        Err e ->
                            Err e

                        Ok renameEntries ->
                            case foldIndexes r.table r.currentColumns r.indexes of
                                Err e ->
                                    Err e

                                Ok indexEntries ->
                                    Ok (createEntry :: renameEntries ++ indexEntries)


foldOps : String -> List SchemaOp -> Result Error (List Db.Migration)
foldOps tableAtCreate ops =
    case ops of
        [] ->
            Ok []

        first :: rest ->
            case opToEntry tableAtCreate first of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok entry ->
                    case foldOps (opNextTable tableAtCreate first) rest of
                        Err e ->
                            Err e

                        Ok more ->
                            Ok (entry :: more)


opNextTable : String -> SchemaOp -> String
opNextTable current op =
    case op of
        RenameTable _ to ->
            to

        RenameColumn _ _ ->
            current


opToEntry : String -> SchemaOp -> Result Error Db.Migration
opToEntry table op =
    case op of
        RenameColumn from to ->
            if from == to then
                Err noopRenameError

            else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent table) then
                Err (invalidIdentError "table" table)

            else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent from) then
                Err (invalidIdentError "column" from)

            else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent to) then
                Err (invalidIdentError "column" to)

            else
                Ok (renameColumnEntry table from to)

        RenameTable from to ->
            if from == to then
                Err noopRenameError

            else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent from) then
                Err (invalidIdentError "table" from)

            else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent to) then
                Err (invalidIdentError "table" to)

            else
                Ok (renameTableEntry from to)


-- The single internal constructor for rename DDL text — the only new
-- SQL-text construction site. Both `renameColumn` and `renameTable` entries
-- are built here, exclusively from already-validated identifiers.
renameColumnEntry : String -> String -> String -> Db.Migration
renameColumnEntry table from to =
    { name =
        String.concat
            [ "rename_column_", table, "_", from, "_to_", to ]
    , sql =
        String.concat
            [ "ALTER TABLE ", table, " RENAME COLUMN ", from, " TO ", to ]
    }


renameTableEntry : String -> String -> Db.Migration
renameTableEntry from to =
    { name =
        String.concat
            [ "rename_table_", from, "_to_", to ]
    , sql =
        String.concat
            [ "ALTER TABLE ", from, " RENAME TO ", to ]
    }


-- ─── Index DDL builders ────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Fold the store's declared indexes to `CREATE INDEX` ledger entries. Every
-- identifier — the index name (derived or caller-supplied), the table name, and
-- each column name — passes through `validSqlIdent` before it reaches the DDL
-- string, and each column is also checked against the store's current column
-- list (parse-don't-validate: a column absent from the codec is an `Err`, never
-- silently dropped). No values are involved (DDL only) — there is no parameter
-- path for indexes.


-- Fold the store's index specs to an ordered list of ledger entries, or fail
-- closed on the first invalid identifier or unknown column.
foldIndexes : String -> List Column -> List IndexSpec -> Result Error (List Db.Migration)
foldIndexes table columns indexes =
    case indexes of
        [] ->
            Ok []

        first :: rest ->
            case indexToEntry table columns first of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok entry ->
                    case foldIndexes table columns rest of
                        Err e ->
                            Err e

                        Ok more ->
                            Ok (entry :: more)


-- Validate and build the `CREATE INDEX` ledger entry for one `IndexSpec`.
-- The table name, the index name (derived or supplied), and every column name
-- all pass through `validSqlIdent`; each column is also checked against the
-- store's current column list (a column absent from the codec is an `Err`).
indexToEntry : String -> List Column -> IndexSpec -> Result Error Db.Migration
indexToEntry table columns spec =
    case spec of
        Index cols ->
            let
                name =
                    String.concat
                        [ "idx_"
                        , table
                        , "_"
                        , String.join "_" cols
                        ]
            in
            buildIndexEntry table name cols columns

        IndexNamed name cols ->
            buildIndexEntry table name cols columns


-- Build a `CREATE INDEX` entry from already-name-resolved components, running
-- every identifier through `validSqlIdent` and each column through the store's
-- column list before constructing the DDL string. Fails on the first invalid or
-- unknown identifier with no partial SQL produced.
buildIndexEntry : String -> String -> List String -> List Column -> Result Error Db.Migration
buildIndexEntry table name cols storeColumns =
    if Basics.not (validSqlIdent table) then
        Err (invalidIdentError "table" table)

    else if Basics.not (validSqlIdent name) then
        Err (invalidIdentError "index" name)

    else if List.isEmpty cols then
        Err emptyIndexError

    else
        case firstInvalidIndexColumn storeColumns cols of
            Just bad ->
                Err bad

            Nothing ->
                Ok
                    { name = String.concat [ "create_index_", name ]
                    , sql =
                        String.concat
                            [ "CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS "
                            , name
                            , " ON "
                            , table
                            , " ("
                            , String.join ", " cols
                            , ")"
                            ]
                    }


-- Check the first column in `cols` that either fails `validSqlIdent` or is
-- absent from the store's column list. Returns `Just (Err e)` for the first
-- bad column, `Nothing` when all are valid and present.
firstInvalidIndexColumn : List Column -> List String -> Maybe Error
firstInvalidIndexColumn storeColumns cols =
    case cols of
        [] ->
            Nothing

        name :: rest ->
            if Basics.not (validSqlIdent name) then
                Just (invalidIdentError "column" name)

            else if Basics.not (hasColumn storeColumns name) then
                Just (unknownColumnError name)

            else
                firstInvalidIndexColumn storeColumns rest


emptyIndexError : Error
emptyIndexError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: an index must name at least one column"


-- ─── Introspection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | The store's current table name (reflects any `renameTable` ops applied).
tableName : Store a -> String
tableName store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            r.table


-- | The store's current column names, in declaration order (reflects any
-- `renameColumn` ops applied).
columnNames : Store a -> List String
columnNames store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            List.map columnName r.currentColumns


-- ─── Write path ────────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `insert conn store row` — insert one row and return its generated id. The
-- row is projected to `(column, SqlValue)` binds through the store's codec
-- (`Ipe.Db.Codec.codecToBinds`); each binds as a typed parameter. Columns
-- marked `Serial` or `DefaultNow` are OMITTED from the insert so the database
-- fills them (a client value produced for such a column is dropped, never
-- written). Routes through the audited `Db.insertFields`, which re-validates
-- every identifier and binds every value — no SQL text is built here. A codec
-- projection that fails (a shape mismatch) is a typed `Err`.
insert : Db -> Store a -> a -> Task Error Int
insert conn store row =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                Err e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Ok binds ->
                    Db.insertFields conn r.table (insertFields r.specs binds)


insertFields : List ColumnSpec -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlField )
insertFields specs values =
    List.map (\pair -> insertField specs pair) values


insertField : List ColumnSpec -> ( String, SqlValue ) -> ( String, SqlField )
insertField specs pair =
    case pair of
        ( name, value ) ->
            if dbFilledColumn specs name then
                ( name, OmitField )

            else
                ( name, SetField value )


dbFilledColumn : List ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
dbFilledColumn specs name =
    List.any (\spec -> specOmitsColumn spec name) specs


specOmitsColumn : ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
specOmitsColumn spec name =
    case spec of
        Serial c ->
            c == name

        DefaultNow c ->
            c == name

        TouchOnUpdate c ->
            c == name

        PrimaryKey _ ->
            False

        Unique _ ->
            False

        DefaultText _ _ ->
            False

        DefaultInt _ _ ->
            False


-- | `insertReturning conn store row` — insert one row and return the stored row
-- as decoded by the store's codec, including any DB-assigned columns (such as a
-- `serial` primary key). The same OmitField logic as `insert` omits `Serial` /
-- `DefaultNow` columns from the write so the database fills them; `RETURNING *`
-- reads every column back — the DB-assigned values are present in the result.
-- The returned row is decoded through the store's own codec (the same path `all`
-- / `get` / `findWhere` use), so a schema drift (a missing column, a cell that
-- is not the declared shape) is a typed `Err`, never a wrong value.
--
-- A codec projection that fails, or a RETURNING row that does not decode to the
-- store's type, is a typed `Err`. Fails closed with no SQL on a non-record codec.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias Item =
--         { id : Int, name : String }
--
--     -- `id` is `serial`, so the DB assigns it; `insertReturning` gives it back.
--     addItem : Db -> Store Item -> String -> Task Error Item
--     addItem conn store name =
--         Store.insertReturning conn store { id = 0, name = name }
insertReturning : Db -> Store a -> a -> Task Error a
insertReturning conn store row =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                Err e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Ok binds ->
                    case r.codec of
                        Codec codecR ->
                            Task.andThen
                                (\results ->
                                    case results of
                                        first :: _ ->
                                            Task.succeed first

                                        [] ->
                                            Task.fail insertReturningNoRowError
                                )
                                (Db.insertFieldsReturning
                                    conn
                                    r.table
                                    (insertFields r.specs binds)
                                    "*"
                                    (codecR.mkDec {})
                                )


insertReturningNoRowError : Error
insertReturningNoRowError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: insertReturning did not receive a RETURNING row from the database"


-- | `update conn store row` — update the row whose primary key matches `row`'s
-- primary-key field, setting every non-generated column from `row`, and return
-- the updated-row count. The whole record is projected through the store's codec
-- (`Ipe.Db.Codec.codecToBinds`); the primary-key bind scopes the UPDATE (its
-- own validated `pk` column, never a caller identifier), and columns marked
-- `Serial` or `DefaultNow` are OMITTED from the SET so a client value can never
-- overwrite a DB-filled column. Routes through the audited `Db.updateFields`,
-- which re-validates every identifier, binds every value, and refuses an
-- unscoped UPDATE — no SQL text is built here. A store with no primary key, or a
-- record whose primary-key column is absent from the codec projection, fails
-- closed with a typed `Err`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     renameUser : Db -> Store User -> User -> Task Error Int
--     renameUser conn store user =
--         Store.update conn store { user | name = "renamed" }
update : Db -> Store a -> a -> Task Error Int
update conn store row =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case r.pk of
                Nothing ->
                    Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                Just keyCol ->
                    case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                        Err e ->
                            Task.fail e

                        Ok binds ->
                            case bindValue keyCol binds of
                                Nothing ->
                                    Task.fail (missingKeyBindError keyCol)

                                Just keyValue ->
                                    Db.updateFields conn
                                        r.table
                                        [ ( keyCol, keyValue ) ]
                                        (updateSetFields r.specs keyCol binds)


-- Project the codec binds to the UPDATE SET fields: the primary-key column is
-- dropped (it scopes the WHERE, never the SET), and each `Serial` / `DefaultNow`
-- column becomes `OmitField` so the database keeps its own value.
updateSetFields : List ColumnSpec -> String -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlField )
updateSetFields specs keyCol values =
    List.map (\pair -> updateSetField specs pair)
        (List.filter (\pair -> Basics.not (bindNameIs keyCol pair)) values)


updateSetField : List ColumnSpec -> ( String, SqlValue ) -> ( String, SqlField )
updateSetField specs pair =
    case pair of
        ( name, value ) ->
            if dbFilledColumn specs name then
                ( name, OmitField )

            else
                ( name, SetField value )


bindNameIs : String -> ( String, SqlValue ) -> Bool
bindNameIs target pair =
    case pair of
        ( name, _ ) ->
            name == target


-- The `SqlValue` bound to `target` in a codec projection, or `Nothing` when the
-- projection names no such column.
bindValue : String -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> Maybe SqlValue
bindValue target binds =
    case binds of
        [] ->
            Nothing

        first :: rest ->
            case first of
                ( name, value ) ->
                    if name == target then
                        Just value

                    else
                        bindValue target rest


-- | `updateWhere conn store cond row` — set every non-DB-filled, non-key column
-- of every row matching `cond` from `row`, and return the updated-row count. The
-- WHERE is `cond` lowered to an audited `SqlFragment` against the store's own
-- columns (an unknown column fails closed with a typed `Err`, exactly as `where`
-- does), so no caller identifier or value reaches the SQL text — every value
-- binds as a parameter. The SET is `row` projected through the store's codec
-- (`Ipe.Db.Codec.codecToBinds`); columns marked `Serial` / `DefaultNow` /
-- `touchOnUpdate` are OMITTED so a client value can never overwrite a DB-filled
-- column, and the primary key is OMITTED too — a bulk update refines existing
-- rows, it never rewrites their identity (which would collapse every matching
-- row onto one key). Routes through the audited `Db.updateWhere`, which
-- re-validates the table and SET columns, binds every value, and refuses an
-- unscoped UPDATE.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     renameAdults : Db -> Store User -> User -> Task Error Int
--     renameAdults conn store template =
--         Store.updateWhere conn store (Store.eq .active True) template
updateWhere : Db -> Store a -> Cond a -> a -> Task Error Int
updateWhere conn store cond row =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            if Basics.not (condConstrainsColumn cond) then
                Task.fail unconstrainedMutationError

            else
                case condFragmentIn r.currentColumns cond of
                    Err e ->
                        Task.fail e

                    Ok frag ->
                        case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                            Err e ->
                                Task.fail e

                            Ok binds ->
                                Db.updateWhere conn
                                    r.table
                                    (updateWhereSetFields r.specs r.pk binds)
                                    frag


-- Project the codec binds to the UPDATE SET fields for a WHERE-scoped update:
-- every DB-filled column (`Serial` / `DefaultNow` / `touchOnUpdate`) becomes
-- `OmitField` so the database keeps its own value, the primary key is dropped
-- entirely (a WHERE-scoped update never rewrites row identity), and every other
-- column is a bound `SetField`.
updateWhereSetFields : List ColumnSpec -> Maybe String -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlField )
updateWhereSetFields specs pk values =
    List.map (\pair -> updateSetField specs pair)
        (List.filter (\pair -> Basics.not (bindIsKey pk pair)) values)


bindIsKey : Maybe String -> ( String, SqlValue ) -> Bool
bindIsKey pk pair =
    case pk of
        Just keyCol ->
            bindNameIs keyCol pair

        Nothing ->
            False


-- | `deleteWhere conn store cond` — delete every row matching `cond` and return
-- the deleted-row count. The WHERE is `cond` lowered to an audited `SqlFragment`
-- against the store's own columns (an unknown column fails closed with a typed
-- `Err`, exactly as `where` does); no caller identifier or value reaches the SQL
-- text. Routes through the audited `Db.deleteWhere`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     purgeMinors : Db -> Store User -> Task Error Int
--     purgeMinors conn store =
--         Store.deleteWhere conn store (Store.eq .active False)
deleteWhere : Db -> Store a -> Cond a -> Task Error Int
deleteWhere conn store cond =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            if Basics.not (condConstrainsColumn cond) then
                Task.fail unconstrainedMutationError

            else
                case condFragmentIn r.currentColumns cond of
                    Err e ->
                        Task.fail e

                    Ok frag ->
                        Db.deleteWhere conn r.table frag


-- Whether `cond` constrains at least one column: True when its `Cond` tree
-- contains any leaf (`Compare` / `Like` / `IsNull` / `NotNull` / `InList`, each
-- of which names a store column), False when it is only connectives over no
-- leaves. `and []` (→ `(1 = 1)`, matches every row), `or []` (→ `(1 = 0)`), and
-- `not` / `and` / `or` nests that bottom out in empty lists all constrain no
-- column. The mutation surface (`updateWhere` / `deleteWhere`) refuses such an
-- unconstrained condition, so a dynamically-empty condition list can never
-- silently become an update-all / delete-all. The read path keeps the all-rows
-- semantics — an unconstrained query is non-destructive.
condConstrainsColumn : Cond row -> Bool
condConstrainsColumn cond =
    case cond of
        Compare _ _ _ ->
            True

        Like _ _ ->
            True

        IsNull _ ->
            True

        NotNull _ ->
            True

        InList _ _ ->
            True

        AndList conds ->
            List.any condConstrainsColumn conds

        OrList conds ->
            List.any condConstrainsColumn conds

        NotCond inner ->
            condConstrainsColumn inner


-- ─── Read path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `all conn store` — read every row and decode each through the store's
-- codec. Routes through `Db.findWhere` with an always-true predicate, so the
-- table name is kernel-validated and no raw SQL is built. A row that does not
-- decode to the store's type is a typed `Err`, never a wrong value.
all : Db -> Store a -> Task Error (List a)
all conn store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Task.andThen
                (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                (Db.findWhere conn r.table alwaysTrue)


-- | `findWhere conn store cond` — read the rows matching `cond` and decode each
-- through the store's codec. `cond` is a `SqlFragment` built from the
-- `Ipe.Db.Sql` combinators: every column flows through `Sql.column`
-- (validated), every value through `Sql.param` (bound). Store adds no injection
-- surface over the audited fragment builder.
findWhere : Db -> Store a -> SqlFragment -> Task Error (List a)
findWhere conn store cond =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Task.andThen
                (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                (Db.findWhere conn r.table cond)


-- | `get conn store keyValue` — read the single row whose primary key equals
-- `keyValue`, or `Nothing`, decoded through the store's codec. Keys on the
-- store's own validated `pk` column, never a caller identifier; the value binds
-- as a parameter. A store with no primary key fails closed with a typed error.
get : Db -> Store a -> SqlValue -> Task Error (Maybe a)
get conn store keyValue =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case r.pk of
                Nothing ->
                    Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                Just keyCol ->
                    Task.andThen
                        (\rows -> firstDecoded r.codec rows)
                        (Db.findWhere conn r.table (keyEquals keyCol keyValue))


-- | `findBy conn store col value` — read every row whose `col` equals `value`,
-- decoded through the store's codec. `col` is validated against the store's own
-- derived column list before any SQL is built (a name absent from the codec's
-- columns is a typed `Err`, parse-don't-validate), then reaches SQL only through
-- `Sql.column`; `value` binds as a parameter through `Sql.param`. This is the
-- convenience single-column equality read; a compound predicate uses the query
-- builder (`query |> where …`) or the raw-fragment escape `findWhere`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     adults : Db -> Store User -> Task Error (List User)
--     adults conn store =
--         Store.findBy conn store "age" (SqlInt 18)
findBy : Db -> Store a -> String -> SqlValue -> Task Error (List a)
findBy conn store col value =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            if Basics.not (hasColumn r.currentColumns col) then
                Task.fail (unknownColumnError col)

            else
                Task.andThen
                    (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                    (Db.findWhere conn r.table (keyEquals col value))


-- Whether `name` is one of the store's current columns — the derived vocabulary
-- a stringly column name must belong to before it can reach SQL.
hasColumn : List Column -> String -> Bool
hasColumn columns name =
    List.any (\col -> columnName col == name) columns


-- ─── External-connection reads (foreign DB through one codec) ───────
--
-- The `…On` reads take an external `Connection mode` (mode-polymorphic: a read
-- is available on `ReadOnly` and `ReadWrite` alike) instead of the app's `Db`,
-- so a monitoring/ETL job reads a foreign source of a DIFFERENT dialect through
-- the SAME store codec. They route through `Db.findWhereOn` over a `Sql.*`-built
-- `SqlFragment` — identical validated-identifier + bound-parameter barrier as
-- the app reads, run against the external pool. There is no external write path:
-- a foreign write is unrepresentable on `Connection ReadOnly` by type.


-- | `allOn conn store` — read every row from an external connection and decode
-- each through the store's codec. The external counterpart to `all`.
allOn : Connection mode -> Store a -> Task Error (List a)
allOn conn store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Task.andThen
                (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                (Db.findWhereOn conn r.table alwaysTrue)


-- | `findWhereOn conn store cond` — read the rows matching `cond` from an
-- external connection and decode each through the store's codec. The external
-- counterpart to `findWhere`; the same audited `SqlFragment` barrier applies.
findWhereOn : Connection mode -> Store a -> SqlFragment -> Task Error (List a)
findWhereOn conn store cond =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            Task.andThen
                (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                (Db.findWhereOn conn r.table cond)


-- | `getOn conn store keyValue` — read the single row whose primary key equals
-- `keyValue` from an external connection, or `Nothing`, decoded through the
-- store's codec. The external counterpart to `get`; keys on the store's
-- validated `pk`, binds the value as a parameter, and fails closed for a store
-- with no primary key.
getOn : Connection mode -> Store a -> SqlValue -> Task Error (Maybe a)
getOn conn store keyValue =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case r.pk of
                Nothing ->
                    Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                Just keyCol ->
                    Task.andThen
                        (\rows -> firstDecoded r.codec rows)
                        (Db.findWhereOn conn r.table (keyEquals keyCol keyValue))


-- | `delete conn store keyValue` — delete the row whose primary key equals
-- `keyValue` and return the deleted-row count. Keys on the validated `pk`
-- column; the value binds as a parameter. A store with no primary key fails
-- closed.
delete : Db -> Store a -> SqlValue -> Task Error Int
delete conn store keyValue =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            case r.pk of
                Nothing ->
                    Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                Just keyCol ->
                    Db.deleteWhere conn r.table (keyEquals keyCol keyValue)


keyEquals : String -> SqlValue -> SqlFragment
keyEquals keyCol keyValue =
    Sql.eq (Sql.column keyCol) (Sql.param keyValue)


-- | An always-true fragment (`1 = 1`): the predicate for an unfiltered read. It
-- names no caller identifier, so it introduces no injection surface.
alwaysTrue : SqlFragment
alwaysTrue =
    Sql.eq (Sql.param (SqlInt 1)) (Sql.param (SqlInt 1))


decodeRows : Codec a -> List Row -> Task Error (List a)
decodeRows codec rows =
    case rows of
        [] ->
            Task.succeed []

        first :: rest ->
            case DbCodec.codecFromRow codec first of
                Err e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Ok value ->
                    Task.map (\more -> value :: more) (decodeRows codec rest)


firstDecoded : Codec a -> List Row -> Task Error (Maybe a)
firstDecoded codec rows =
    case rows of
        [] ->
            Task.succeed Nothing

        first :: _ ->
            case DbCodec.codecFromRow codec first of
                Err e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Ok value ->
                    Task.succeed (Just value)


-- ─── Typed query builder (Cond → SqlFragment) ──────────────────────
--
-- A `Query a` is a filtered, ordered, paginated read over a `Store a`. Its
-- `WHERE` is a `Cond`: a closed ADT of typed leaves (a validated column name +
-- a bound `SqlValue`) that lowers, only at the terminal `toList` / `toMaybe` /
-- `count`, to the audited `SqlFragment` — every column through `Sql.column`,
-- every value through `Sql.param`. No leaf builds SQL text, so the builder adds
-- no injection surface over the fragment builder it lowers to. A column named in
-- a leaf is validated against the store's own derived column list at lowering
-- time (a name absent from the codec's columns is a typed `Err`), so a stringly
-- column that is not a real store column fails closed before any SQL is issued.
--
-- `ORDER BY` / `LIMIT` / `OFFSET` are applied in Ipê over the fetched rows: the
-- `WHERE` runs at the SQL layer (the injection-safe fragment), and the fetched
-- rows are then sorted by the ordered columns (numeric-aware for `CInt` / `CReal`
-- columns, lexicographic otherwise, per the store's own column types) and sliced.
-- No ordering identifier ever reaches SQL text, so the ordered surface adds no
-- injection surface either.


-- | A comparison operator carried by a query leaf. A typed ADT, not a stringly
-- operator, so a leaf can only name one of the six comparisons the fragment
-- builder provides.
type CompareOp
    = OpEq
    | OpNeq
    | OpGt
    | OpGte
    | OpLt
    | OpLte


-- | A typed `WHERE` predicate over a `Store row`'s rows. The `row` parameter
-- ties the predicate to the row type it queries: an accessor-typed leaf
-- (`eq .age 18`) fixes `row` to the store's row type, so a column from one row
-- type cannot be used against another, and a value's type must match the field's.
-- Built only through the leaf combinators (`eq` / `eqBy`) and the connectives
-- (`and` / `or` / `not`); it carries a validated column name and a bound value as
-- pure data and lowers to a `SqlFragment` at query time. The `row` parameter is a
-- phantom over the data — the constructors carry only the concrete column/value —
-- so the runtime `Cond` is untyped data while the surface stays row-checked.
type Cond row
    = Compare CompareOp String SqlValue
    | Like String String
    | IsNull String
    | NotNull String
    | InList String (List SqlValue)
    | AndList (List (Cond row))
    | OrList (List (Cond row))
    | NotCond (Cond row)


-- | A filtered, ordered, paginated read over a `Store a`, decoded through the
-- store's codec at `toList` / `toMaybe` / `count`. Built through `query` and
-- refined by `where` / `orderAsc` / `orderDesc` / `limit` / `offset`. Opaque:
-- the store it reads and the codec it decodes with cannot drift, because both
-- come from the single `Store a` `query` captured.
--
-- `frag` is the accumulated `WHERE` fragment: each `where` lowers its `Cond` to
-- an audited `SqlFragment` EAGERLY (parse-don't-validate — the store is in hand,
-- so every column is validated against the store's own columns at that moment)
-- and conjoins it. `orderings` carries each ordering column as
-- `(name, isNumeric, ascending)`, `isNumeric` resolved from the store's column
-- type when the ordering is added, so the raw-row sort compares numerically for
-- `CInt` / `CReal` columns without re-consulting the store. `poison` holds the
-- first fail-closed error (an unknown column named by a `where` leaf or an
-- ordering); a poisoned query issues no SQL and fails at the terminal step.
type Query a
    = Query
        { store : Store a
        , frag : Maybe SqlFragment
        , orderings : List ( String, Bool, Bool )
        , lim : Maybe Int
        , off : Maybe Int
        , poison : Maybe Error
        }


-- | `query store` — begin a read over `store` with no filter, ordering, or
-- pagination. Refine it with `where` / `orderAsc` / `orderDesc` / `limit` /
-- `offset`, then run it with `toList` / `toMaybe` / `count`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias User =
--         { id : String, name : String, age : Int }
--
--     topAdults : Db -> Store User -> Task Error (List User)
--     topAdults conn store =
--         Store.query store
--             |> Store.where (Store.eq .age 18)
--             |> Store.orderDesc "age"
--             |> Store.limit 10
--             |> Store.toList conn
query : Store a -> Query a
query store =
    Query
        { store = store
        , frag = Nothing
        , orderings = []
        , lim = Nothing
        , off = Nothing
        , poison = Nothing
        }


-- | `where cond q` — restrict `q` to the rows matching `cond`. Applying `where`
-- more than once AND-joins the predicates, so a pipeline of `where` clauses
-- reads as a conjunction.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .status "active")
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .active True)
where : Cond a -> Query a -> Query a
where cond q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            case r.store of
                Store s ->
                    case condFragmentIn s.currentColumns cond of
                        Err e ->
                            Query
                                { store = r.store
                                , frag = r.frag
                                , orderings = r.orderings
                                , lim = r.lim
                                , off = r.off
                                , poison = firstPoison r.poison e
                                }

                        Ok lowered ->
                            Query
                                { store = r.store
                                , frag = Just (conjoinFrag r.frag lowered)
                                , orderings = r.orderings
                                , lim = r.lim
                                , off = r.off
                                , poison = r.poison
                                }


conjoinFrag : Maybe SqlFragment -> SqlFragment -> SqlFragment
conjoinFrag existing added =
    case existing of
        Nothing ->
            added

        Just prior ->
            Sql.and prior added


-- Keep the FIRST fail-closed error a query accrues: a later error does not mask
-- the earliest cause.
firstPoison : Maybe Error -> Error -> Maybe Error
firstPoison existing e =
    case existing of
        Just _ ->
            existing

        Nothing ->
            Just e


-- ── Comparison leaf ────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `eq accessor value` — the rows where the record column named by the field
-- `accessor` equals `value`. The column is named by an accessor literal (e.g.
-- `.age`), so it is checked against the row type at compile time; the value's
-- type is pinned to the field's type, so a mismatched literal is a type error.
-- The column identifier is derived and validated at lowering, and `value` binds
-- as a parameter — never interpolated.
--
-- The field type must be a scalar (`String` / `Int` / `Bool` / `Float`). A
-- column whose field is an enum or newtype needs its codec: use `eqBy`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .age 18)
eq : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
eq =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_eq"


-- | `eqBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed equality leaf for an ENUM
-- or newtype column, whose wire form is not type-derivable. `codec` is the
-- field's own codec: `value` is projected through it to a bound `SqlValue`, so
-- the stored wire form and the query's bound value agree by construction. The
-- column is named by the accessor literal (checked against the row type) and
-- validated at lowering; `value` binds as a parameter.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eqBy rankCodec .rank Gold)
eqBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
eqBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_eqBy"


-- The lowered form of `eqBy`: the accessor `.field` (checked against the row
-- type at compile time) has already been turned into the validated column name
-- `col`, so this builds the value-carrying `Cond`. `codec` projects `v` to one
-- bound `SqlValue` through `Ipe.Db.Codec.toSqlValue` (a scalar-shaped codec — an
-- enum or newtype), which the `Compare` leaf later lowers to `Sql.param`. A codec
-- that is not scalar-shaped, or a value that does not encode to its declared
-- `ColType`, fails closed to the always-false `Cond` (`OrList []`) — the query
-- matches no row rather than binding a wrong value.
--
-- Reached only through the `Store.eqBy` lowering intercept, which supplies `col`;
-- not exported, so `col` is never a caller-supplied string.
eqByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
eqByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpEq col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `neq accessor value` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is
-- NOT equal to `value`. Scalar fields only; for an enum or newtype column use
-- `neqBy`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.neq .status "deleted")
neq : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
neq =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_neq"


-- | `neqBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed not-equal leaf for an
-- enum or newtype column. Projects `value` through `codec` to a bound
-- `SqlValue`; the column is named by the accessor literal.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.neqBy rankCodec .rank Gold)
neqBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
neqBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_neqBy"


neqByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
neqByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpNeq col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `gt accessor value` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is
-- greater than `value`. Scalar fields only; for an enum or newtype column use
-- `gtBy`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.gt .age 18)
gt : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
gt =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_gt"


-- | `gtBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed greater-than leaf for an
-- enum or newtype column.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.gtBy priceCodec .price minPrice)
gtBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
gtBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_gtBy"


gtByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
gtByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpGt col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `gte accessor value` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is
-- greater than or equal to `value`. Scalar fields only; use `gteBy` for enum
-- or newtype columns.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.gte .age 18)
gte : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
gte =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_gte"


-- | `gteBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed greater-than-or-equal
-- leaf for an enum or newtype column.
gteBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
gteBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_gteBy"


gteByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
gteByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpGte col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `lt accessor value` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is
-- less than `value`. Scalar fields only; use `ltBy` for enum or newtype
-- columns.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.lt .age 65)
lt : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
lt =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_lt"


-- | `ltBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed less-than leaf for an
-- enum or newtype column.
ltBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
ltBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_ltBy"


ltByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
ltByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpLt col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `lte accessor value` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is
-- less than or equal to `value`. Scalar fields only; use `lteBy` for enum or
-- newtype columns.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.lte .age 64)
lte : (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
lte =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_lte"


-- | `lteBy codec accessor value` — the accessor-typed less-than-or-equal leaf
-- for an enum or newtype column.
lteBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> value -> Cond row
lteBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_lteBy"


lteByNamed : String -> Codec value -> value -> Cond row
lteByNamed col codec v =
    case DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v of
        Ok sqlValue ->
            Compare OpLte col sqlValue

        Err _ ->
            OrList []


-- | `like accessor pattern` — the rows where the `String` column named by
-- `accessor` matches the SQL `LIKE` `pattern`. The pattern binds as a
-- parameter (wildcards `%` / `_` are DATA the SQL engine interprets, never
-- SQL text the caller injects). The accessor must name a `String` field.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.like .name "al%")
like : (row -> String) -> String -> Cond row
like =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_like"


-- | `isNull accessor` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is SQL
-- NULL. The accessor names the column; no value argument.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.isNull .deletedAt)
isNull : (row -> t) -> Cond row
isNull =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_isNull"


-- | `notNull accessor` — the rows where the column named by `accessor` is NOT
-- SQL NULL.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.notNull .email)
notNull : (row -> t) -> Cond row
notNull =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_notNull"


-- | `inList accessor values` — the rows where the column named by `accessor`
-- is one of the scalar `values`. Each value binds as a parameter. An empty
-- list matches no row (`(1 = 0)`, the identity of disjunction).
--
-- The field type must be scalar (`String` / `Int` / `Bool` / `Float`). For
-- an enum or newtype column use `inListBy`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.inList .status [ "active", "trial" ])
inList : (row -> value) -> List value -> Cond row
inList =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_inList"


-- | `inListBy codec accessor values` — the accessor-typed IN-list leaf for an
-- enum or newtype column. Each element in `values` is projected through
-- `codec` to a bound `SqlValue`; elements that fail the projection are
-- dropped (the remaining elements still form the IN clause). An empty or
-- all-dropped list matches no row (`OrList []`).
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.inListBy rankCodec .rank [ Gold, Silver ])
inListBy : Codec value -> (row -> value) -> List value -> Cond row
inListBy =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_inListBy"


-- The lowered form of `inListBy`: the accessor `.field` has already been
-- turned into the validated column name `col`. Each element of `values` is
-- projected through `codec`; elements that fail the projection are dropped.
-- An empty result (no values survive projection) lowers to the always-false
-- `Cond` (`OrList []`).
--
-- Reached only through the `Store.inListBy` lowering intercept, which
-- supplies `col`; not exported.
inListByNamed : String -> Codec value -> List value -> Cond row
inListByNamed col codec values =
    let
        sqlValues =
            List.filterMap (\v -> Result.toMaybe (DbCodec.toSqlValue codec v)) values
    in
    if List.isEmpty sqlValues then
        OrList []

    else
        InList col sqlValues


-- The lowered form of `inList` (scalar): the accessor `.field` has been
-- turned into the validated column name `col`, and `sqlValues` is the
-- already-mapped `List SqlValue`. An empty list lowers to the always-false
-- `Cond`.
--
-- Reached only through the `Store.inList` lowering intercept; not exported.
inListNamed : String -> List SqlValue -> Cond row
inListNamed col sqlValues =
    if List.isEmpty sqlValues then
        OrList []

    else
        InList col sqlValues


-- ── Connectives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `and conds` — the rows matching EVERY condition in `conds`. An empty list
-- matches every row (the identity of conjunction, `(1 = 1)`).
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where
--             (Store.and
--                 [ Store.eq .active True
--                 , Store.eq .status "active"
--                 ]
--             )
and : List (Cond row) -> Cond row
and conds =
    AndList conds


-- | `or conds` — the rows matching ANY condition in `conds`. An empty list
-- matches no row (the identity of disjunction, `(1 = 0)`).
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where
--             (Store.or
--                 [ Store.eq .status "active"
--                 , Store.eq .status "trial"
--                 ]
--             )
or : List (Cond row) -> Cond row
or conds =
    OrList conds


-- | `not cond` — the rows NOT matching `cond`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.not (Store.eq .status "deleted"))
not : Cond row -> Cond row
not cond =
    NotCond cond


-- ── Ordering and pagination ────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `orderAsc col q` — order `q`'s rows by `col` ascending. Repeated ordering
-- clauses compose left to right (the first is the primary sort key). The column
-- is validated against the store's columns at query time.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.orderAsc "name"
orderAsc : String -> Query a -> Query a
orderAsc col q =
    addOrdering col True q


-- | `orderDesc col q` — order `q`'s rows by `col` descending.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.orderDesc "age"
orderDesc : String -> Query a -> Query a
orderDesc col q =
    addOrdering col False q


addOrdering : String -> Bool -> Query a -> Query a
addOrdering col ascending q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            case r.store of
                Store s ->
                    case columnTypeOf s.currentColumns col of
                        Nothing ->
                            Query
                                { store = r.store
                                , frag = r.frag
                                , orderings = r.orderings
                                , lim = r.lim
                                , off = r.off
                                , poison = firstPoison r.poison (unknownColumnError col)
                                }

                        Just colType ->
                            Query
                                { store = r.store
                                , frag = r.frag
                                , orderings = r.orderings ++ [ ( col, isNumericType colType, ascending ) ]
                                , lim = r.lim
                                , off = r.off
                                , poison = r.poison
                                }


-- | `limit n q` — keep at most `n` rows (applied after ordering). A negative or
-- zero `n` keeps no rows.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.orderDesc "age"
--         |> Store.limit 10
limit : Int -> Query a -> Query a
limit n q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            Query
                { store = r.store
                , frag = r.frag
                , orderings = r.orderings
                , lim = Just n
                , off = r.off
                , poison = r.poison
                }


-- | `offset n q` — skip the first `n` rows (applied after ordering, before
-- `limit`). A negative or zero `n` skips none.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.orderAsc "name"
--         |> Store.offset 20
--         |> Store.limit 10
offset : Int -> Query a -> Query a
offset n q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            Query
                { store = r.store
                , frag = r.frag
                , orderings = r.orderings
                , lim = r.lim
                , off = Just n
                , poison = r.poison
                }


-- ── Running a query ────────────────────────────────────────────────


-- | `toList conn q` — run `q` and decode every matching row through the store's
-- codec. The `WHERE` runs as an audited `SqlFragment` at the SQL layer; the
-- fetched rows are ordered and paginated in Ipê, then decoded. A row that does
-- not decode to the store's type, or a column name that is not one of the
-- store's columns, is a typed `Err`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .age 18)
--         |> Store.orderDesc "age"
--         |> Store.limit 10
--         |> Store.toList conn
toList : Db -> Query a -> Task Error (List a)
toList conn q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            case r.poison of
                Just e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Nothing ->
                    case r.store of
                        Store s ->
                            Task.andThen
                                (\rows -> decodeRows s.codec (paginateRows r.orderings r.off r.lim rows))
                                (Db.findWhere conn s.table (queryFrag r.frag))


-- The `WHERE` fragment a query runs: its accumulated fragment, or the
-- always-true `(1 = 1)` when no `where` was applied (an unfiltered read). Names
-- no caller identifier in the empty case, so it adds no injection surface.
queryFrag : Maybe SqlFragment -> SqlFragment
queryFrag maybeFrag =
    case maybeFrag of
        Just frag ->
            frag

        Nothing ->
            trueFragment


-- The ordering key a raw sort needs: the column NAME (to read the cell), whether
-- its store column type is numeric (for a numeric-aware compare), and the
-- ascending flag. Carries no `ColType`, so the `Query` record it lives on
-- references only built-in types.
type alias OrderKey =
    ( String, Bool, Bool )


-- | `toMaybe conn q` — run `q` and decode the FIRST matching row (after ordering
-- and pagination) through the store's codec, or `Nothing`. A `limit 1` is not
-- required; `toMaybe` reads the head of the ordered result.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .email "a@example.com")
--         |> Store.toMaybe conn
toMaybe : Db -> Query a -> Task Error (Maybe a)
toMaybe conn q =
    Task.map List.head (toList conn q)


-- | `count conn q` — run `q`'s filter and return how many rows match. Ordering
-- and pagination do not affect the count of matched rows (the filter alone
-- decides membership).
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.query store
--         |> Store.where (Store.eq .status "active")
--         |> Store.count conn
count : Db -> Query a -> Task Error Int
count conn q =
    case q of
        Query r ->
            case r.poison of
                Just e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Nothing ->
                    case r.store of
                        Store s ->
                            Task.map List.length (Db.findWhere conn s.table (queryFrag r.frag))


columnTypeOf : List Column -> String -> Maybe ColType
columnTypeOf columns name =
    case columns of
        [] ->
            Nothing

        first :: rest ->
            if columnName first == name then
                Just (columnColType first)

            else
                columnTypeOf rest name


-- ─── Typed two-store inner join ─────────────────────────────────────
--
-- A `Joined a b` is an inner join of two stores on key equality, lowering to
-- ONE parameterized `SELECT` over a two-table `FROM` — never a per-row fan-out.
-- It carries both stores (so both codecs are in hand for decode), the two
-- validated join-key columns, and an accumulated joined `WHERE` fragment. The
-- two sides are bound to the fixed aliases `a0` (left) and `a1` (right); every
-- column the join references reaches SQL only as a validated `alias.column`
-- reference through `Sql.column`, and every filter value binds as a parameter
-- through `Sql.param`, so the join adds no injection surface over the audited
-- single-table path. A filter column absent from its side's store fails closed
-- with a typed `Err` carried as `poison`, exactly as the single-table `Query`
-- does. Built only through `join`, so both key columns are already accepted.


-- | The alias bound to the left store in a join (`FROM ta AS a0`).
leftAlias : String
leftAlias =
    "a0"


-- | The alias bound to the right store in a join (`FROM tb AS a1`).
rightAlias : String
rightAlias =
    "a1"


-- | Sort direction for `orderByLeft` / `orderByRight`. `Asc` sorts the join
-- result ascending on the named column; `Desc` sorts descending.
type Order
    = Asc
    | Desc


-- | An inner join of two stores on key equality, decoded through each store's
-- own codec at `joinToList` / `joinToMaybe` / `joinCount`. `storeA` / `storeB`
-- are the two sides (left bound to `a0`, right to `a1`); `keyA` / `keyB` are the
-- validated join-key columns (`a0.keyA = a1.keyB`); `frag` is the accumulated
-- joined `WHERE` fragment (the key equality plus any `joinFilterLeft` /
-- `joinFilterRight`); `order` is an optional `(alias, column, ascending)` triple
-- attached by `orderByLeft` / `orderByRight`; `poison` holds the first
-- fail-closed error (a filter column absent from its side). Opaque: the stores
-- it reads and the codecs it decodes with cannot drift, because both come from
-- the two `Store` values `join` captured.
type Joined a b
    = Joined
        { storeA : Store a
        , storeB : Store b
        , keyA : String
        , keyB : String
        , frag : SqlFragment
        , order : Maybe ( String, String, Bool )
        , poison : Maybe Error
        }


-- | `join storeA .keyA storeB .keyB` — begin an inner join of `storeA` and
-- `storeB` on `a0.keyA = a1.keyB`. The two accessor arguments name the join
-- columns the same way `eq .field` names a column: each `.field` is checked
-- against its store's row type at compile time, and their result type `k` must
-- match, so a mistyped join key is a type error. The lowering intercept extracts
-- each accessor's validated column and calls `joinNamed`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     import Ipe.Db as Db exposing (Db)
--     import Ipe.Db.Store as Store exposing (Store)
--     import Ipe.Task as Task exposing (Task)
--     import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
--
--     type alias Book =
--         { id : String, title : String, authorId : String }
--
--     type alias Author =
--         { id : String, name : String }
--
--     booksByAuthor : Db -> Store Book -> Store Author -> Task Error (List ( Book, Author ))
--     booksByAuthor conn books authors =
--         Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--             |> Store.joinToList conn
join : Store a -> (a -> k) -> Store b -> (b -> k) -> Joined a b
join =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_join"


-- | The lowered form of `join`: each accessor `.field` has already been turned
-- into its validated, snake_cased column name (`keyA` / `keyB`). Builds the
-- `Joined` with the key-equality WHERE fragment `a0.keyA = a1.keyB` — both sides
-- reach SQL only through `Sql.column` on the aliased reference. If either key is
-- somehow not a valid dotted identifier the fragment poisons itself (the
-- `Sql.column` gate), which the runners surface as a typed `Err`; no value is
-- interpolated.
--
-- Reached only through the `Store.join` lowering intercept, which supplies the
-- columns; not part of the accessor caller surface.
joinNamed : Store a -> String -> Store b -> String -> Joined a b
joinNamed storeA keyA storeB keyB =
    Joined
        { storeA = storeA
        , storeB = storeB
        , keyA = keyA
        , keyB = keyB
        , frag = joinKeyEquals keyA keyB
        , order = Nothing
        , poison = Nothing
        }


-- The join-key equality fragment `a0.keyA = a1.keyB`: each side is a validated
-- `alias.column` reference through `Sql.column`, never interpolated text.
joinKeyEquals : String -> String -> SqlFragment
joinKeyEquals keyA keyB =
    Sql.eq
        (Sql.column (qualified leftAlias keyA))
        (Sql.column (qualified rightAlias keyB))


-- Qualify a bare column with a table alias (`a0` + `title` → `a0.title`). The
-- result is a dotted reference `Sql.column` accepts through the same identifier
-- gate the single-table path uses.
qualified : String -> String -> String
qualified alias col =
    String.concat [ alias, ".", col ]


-- | `joinFilterLeft cond joined` — restrict the join to rows whose LEFT side
-- matches `cond`. `cond` is an ordinary `Cond a` over the left store's row type
-- (built with `eq` / `and` / …), so it is row-checked to the left side and its
-- values bind as parameters. Its columns are validated against the left store's
-- own columns and qualified with the left alias (`a0.col`) before reaching SQL,
-- so a column absent from the left store fails closed with a typed `Err`.
-- Repeated filters AND-join.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterLeft (Store.eq .available True)
joinFilterLeft : Cond a -> Joined a b -> Joined a b
joinFilterLeft cond joined =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            case r.storeA of
                Store s ->
                    addJoinFilter leftAlias s.currentColumns cond joined


-- | `joinFilterRight cond joined` — restrict the join to rows whose RIGHT side
-- matches `cond`. The right-side counterpart to `joinFilterLeft`: `cond` is a
-- `Cond b` over the right store's row type, validated against the right store's
-- columns and qualified with the right alias (`a1.col`).
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterRight (Store.eq .active True)
joinFilterRight : Cond b -> Joined a b -> Joined a b
joinFilterRight cond joined =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            case r.storeB of
                Store s ->
                    addJoinFilter rightAlias s.currentColumns cond joined


-- Lower `cond` against one side's columns, qualify each referenced column with
-- that side's alias, and AND it into the join's WHERE fragment. A column absent
-- from the side's store poisons the join with the first fail-closed error, so a
-- poisoned join issues no SQL and fails at the terminal runner.
addJoinFilter : String -> List Column -> Cond row -> Joined a b -> Joined a b
addJoinFilter alias columns cond joined =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            case condFragmentInQualified alias columns cond of
                Err e ->
                    Joined
                        { storeA = r.storeA
                        , storeB = r.storeB
                        , keyA = r.keyA
                        , keyB = r.keyB
                        , frag = r.frag
                        , order = r.order
                        , poison = firstPoison r.poison e
                        }

                Ok lowered ->
                    Joined
                        { storeA = r.storeA
                        , storeB = r.storeB
                        , keyA = r.keyA
                        , keyB = r.keyB
                        , frag = Sql.and r.frag lowered
                        , order = r.order
                        , poison = r.poison
                        }


-- | `orderByLeft accessor order joined` — sort the join result by a column from
-- the LEFT side (`a0`). The accessor names the sort column the same way `eq`
-- names a filter column; lowering extracts the snake_cased column name and
-- rewrites to `orderByLeftNamed`. `Asc` sorts ascending; `Desc` descending.
-- Only the last `orderByLeft` / `orderByRight` call on a `Joined` takes effect.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.orderByLeft .title Asc
--         |> Store.joinToList db
orderByLeft : (a -> k) -> Order -> Joined a b -> Joined a b
orderByLeft =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_orderByLeft"


-- | `orderByRight accessor order joined` — sort the join result by a column from
-- the RIGHT side (`a1`). Symmetric to `orderByLeft`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterRight (Store.eq .active True)
--         |> Store.orderByRight .name Desc
--         |> Store.joinToList db
orderByRight : (b -> k) -> Order -> Joined a b -> Joined a b
orderByRight =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_orderByRight"


-- | Lowered form of `orderByLeft`: the accessor has already been turned into its
-- validated column name and the `Order` into a `Bool`. Attaches the sort
-- specification to the join so `joinToList` routes to `Db.findJoinOrdered`.
-- Reached only through the `Store.orderByLeft` lowering intercept.
orderByLeftNamed : Joined a b -> String -> Bool -> Joined a b
orderByLeftNamed joined col ascending =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            Joined
                { storeA = r.storeA
                , storeB = r.storeB
                , keyA = r.keyA
                , keyB = r.keyB
                , frag = r.frag
                , order = Just ( leftAlias, col, ascending )
                , poison = r.poison
                }


-- | Lowered form of `orderByRight`: symmetric to `orderByLeftNamed` but uses
-- the RIGHT side alias (`a1`). Reached only through the `Store.orderByRight`
-- lowering intercept.
orderByRightNamed : Joined a b -> String -> Bool -> Joined a b
orderByRightNamed joined col ascending =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            Joined
                { storeA = r.storeA
                , storeB = r.storeB
                , keyA = r.keyA
                , keyB = r.keyB
                , frag = r.frag
                , order = Just ( rightAlias, col, ascending )
                , poison = r.poison
                }


-- | `joinToList conn joined` — run the join and return each matched pair, both
-- full rows decoded through their own store's codec. Lowers to ONE
-- parameterized statement (`SELECT … FROM ta AS a0, tb AS a1 WHERE a0.keyA =
-- a1.keyB [AND filters]`) through `Db.findJoin` (or `Db.findJoinOrdered` when
-- `orderByLeft` / `orderByRight` was applied): every identifier (both tables,
-- both aliases, every projected column) is re-validated at the runtime boundary,
-- and every filter value is a bound parameter. A poisoned join (a filter naming
-- an unknown column) fails closed with the typed `Err`; a row that does not
-- decode to its side's type is a typed `Err`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterRight (Store.eq .active True)
--         |> Store.joinToList conn
joinToList : Db -> Joined a b -> Task Error (List ( a, b ))
joinToList conn joined =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            case r.poison of
                Just e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Nothing ->
                    case r.storeA of
                        Store sa ->
                            case r.storeB of
                                Store sb ->
                                    case r.order of
                                        Nothing ->
                                            Task.andThen
                                                (\pairs -> decodeJoinRows sa.codec sb.codec pairs)
                                                (Db.findJoin conn
                                                    sa.table
                                                    leftAlias
                                                    (List.map columnName sa.currentColumns)
                                                    sb.table
                                                    rightAlias
                                                    (List.map columnName sb.currentColumns)
                                                    r.frag
                                                )

                                        Just ( orderAlias, orderCol, orderAsc ) ->
                                            Task.andThen
                                                (\pairs -> decodeJoinRows sa.codec sb.codec pairs)
                                                (Db.findJoinOrdered conn
                                                    sa.table
                                                    leftAlias
                                                    (List.map columnName sa.currentColumns)
                                                    sb.table
                                                    rightAlias
                                                    (List.map columnName sb.currentColumns)
                                                    r.frag
                                                    orderAlias
                                                    orderCol
                                                    orderAsc
                                                )


-- | `joinToMaybe conn joined` — run the join and decode the FIRST matched pair,
-- or `Nothing`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterLeft (Store.eq .id "book-1")
--         |> Store.joinToMaybe conn
joinToMaybe : Db -> Joined a b -> Task Error (Maybe ( a, b ))
joinToMaybe conn joined =
    Task.map List.head (joinToList conn joined)


-- | `joinCount conn joined` — run the join's filter and return how many pairs
-- match. Decodes nothing; counts the matched join rows.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.joinFilterRight (Store.eq .active True)
--         |> Store.joinCount conn
joinCount : Db -> Joined a b -> Task Error Int
joinCount conn joined =
    Task.map List.length (joinToList conn joined)


-- ─── Typed column projection over a join (`select` / `Select`) ───────
--
-- `select` projects specific columns of a join instead of returning both full
-- rows. Its lambda receives the pair of the two sides' row records (`( a, b )`)
-- and returns either a bare field reference on one side
-- (`\( _, author ) -> author.name`) or a flat tuple of such references
-- (`\( book, author ) -> ( book.title, author.name )`). The lambda is never run
-- over real rows: the compiler reads each referenced column from its `side.field`
-- element at lowering, validates it against that side's row type, and sets the
-- SQL `SELECT` list to exactly those columns (column pushdown), over the join's
-- existing `FROM … WHERE`.
--
-- The security posture is the join's, extended: each projected identifier
-- (alias + column) is a validated `alias.column` reference that reaches SQL only
-- through the runtime's identifier gate, and the `select` lambda cannot smuggle a
-- raw value into the SELECT text — a body that is not a column reference (or a
-- flat tuple of column references) is a fail-closed build error (IPE-L0149),
-- never a computed fragment. Each projected column binds to an output name by
-- position (`p0`, `p1`, …); `selectToList` / `selectToMaybe` return the projected
-- `row` directly — the per-column decode is emitted concretely at the call site
-- from the projected shape the `select` lambda fixed, so no decoder is stored in
-- the projection value and the caller sees a typed `row`, never a positional read.


-- | A column projection over a join, returned as the typed `row` by
-- `selectToList` / `selectToMaybe`. `row` is a PHANTOM type argument: it records
-- the projected shape the `select` lambda fixed so the call site emits the right
-- concrete decode, while the runtime value carries only query data. Built only
-- through `select`, so the projected columns and the shape that reads them back
-- cannot drift. `joined` is the underlying join (its `FROM … WHERE` and any
-- `poison`); `projections` is the ordered typed projection descriptor list (one
-- `ProjectionTerm` per SELECT term — see `selectNamed`).
type Select row
    = Select
        { joinedA : String
        , joinedB : String
        , leftTable : String
        , rightTable : String
        , frag : SqlFragment
        , order : Maybe ( String, String, Bool )
        , poison : Maybe Error
        , projections : List ProjectionTerm
        , extraBinds : List SqlValue
        }


-- | `select (\( left, right ) -> projection) joined` — project specific columns
-- of `joined` instead of both full rows. The lambda receives the two sides' row
-- records and returns either a bare field reference on one side or a flat tuple
-- of such references; lowering reads each referenced column and sets the SQL
-- `SELECT` list to exactly those columns. The lambda is never run over real
-- rows, so each field reference names a validated column: a projection that is
-- not a column reference (a computed expression, a literal) — or a tuple with a
-- non-reference or nested-tuple element — is a fail-closed build error, never a
-- raw SQL fragment.
--
-- `selectToList` / `selectToMaybe` return the projected `row` directly: the
-- per-column decode is emitted concretely at the call site from the projected
-- shape this lambda fixed, so a single-column projection yields the scalar and a
-- tuple projection yields the tuple — no positional read in caller code.
--
-- Example:
--
--     -- one column: a `List String`
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.select (\( _, author ) -> author.name)
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--
--     -- two columns: a `List ( String, String )`
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.select (\( book, author ) -> ( book.title, author.name ))
--         |> Store.selectToList db
select : (( a, b ) -> row) -> Joined a b -> Select row
select =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_select"


-- | `literal value` — inside a `Store.select` projection lambda, binds `value`
-- as a SQL parameter (`? AS pN`) rather than naming a column. The value is
-- never interpolated into SQL text; its type picks the concrete reader the decode
-- emits. Valid only as a direct element of a `Store.select` projection body: a
-- point-free or partially-applied `literal` is a fail-closed build error.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.select (\( _, author ) -> ( literal "fiction", author.name ))
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--         -- : Task Error (List ( String, String ))
--         -- SQL: SELECT ? AS p0, a1.name AS p1 FROM …
literal : t -> t
literal =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_literal"


-- | `upper col` — inside a `Store.select` projection lambda, applies SQL
-- `UPPER(…)` to a `String` column reference. Valid only as a direct element of a
-- `Store.select` projection body applied to a `side.field` accessor; the accessor
-- must resolve to a `String` column. A non-column or non-String argument is a
-- fail-closed build error (IPE-L0149). Every column identifier is validated at
-- compile time and re-checked by the runtime.
--
-- Example:
--
--     joinedBooks
--         |> Store.select (\( _, author ) -> upper author.name)
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--         -- SQL: SELECT UPPER(a1.name) AS p0 FROM …
upper : String -> String
upper =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_upper"


-- | `lower col` — symmetric counterpart to `upper`: applies SQL `LOWER(…)` to
-- a `String` column reference. The same structural restrictions apply.
--
-- Example:
--
--     joinedBooks
--         |> Store.select (\( book, _ ) -> lower book.title)
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--         -- SQL: SELECT LOWER(a0.title) AS p0 FROM …
lower : String -> String
lower =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_lower"


-- | `coalesce left right` — inside a `Store.select` projection lambda, emits
-- `COALESCE(a, b) AS pN` in the SELECT list, returning the first non-null
-- value. Both operands must share the same scalar type `a` (the type variable
-- is unified by the constraint scheme). Each operand is either a bare
-- `side.field` column reference or a `Store.literal` value; a computed
-- expression is a fail-closed build error (IPE-L0149). The operand types must
-- agree; a mismatch is a compile-time type error. Every column identifier is
-- validated at compile time and re-checked by the runtime.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.select (\( _, author ) -> coalesce author.name (literal "unknown"))
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--         -- SQL: SELECT COALESCE(a1.name, ?) AS p0 FROM …
coalesce : Projection a -> Projection a -> Projection a
coalesce =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_coalesce"


-- | The lowered form of `select`: the lambda has already been read into the
-- ordered `ProjectionTerm` list. Each term is one typed SELECT descriptor:
-- `ColumnTerm alias col`, `LiteralTerm`, `UpperTerm dotted`, `LowerTerm dotted`,
-- or `CoalesceTerm operandA operandB`. Literal-position `SqlValue` binds appear
-- in `extraBinds` in left→right, coalesce-left-before-right order, ahead of the
-- WHERE binds. Builds the `Select` from the join's tables, alias-bound `FROM …
-- WHERE` fragment, and any inherited `poison`. Reached only through the
-- `Store.select` lowering intercept; not part of the accessor caller surface.
selectNamed : Joined a b -> List ProjectionTerm -> List SqlValue -> Select row
selectNamed joined projections extraBinds =
    case joined of
        Joined r ->
            case r.storeA of
                Store sa ->
                    case r.storeB of
                        Store sb ->
                            Select
                                { joinedA = leftAlias
                                , joinedB = rightAlias
                                , leftTable = sa.table
                                , rightTable = sb.table
                                , frag = r.frag
                                , order = r.order
                                , poison = r.poison
                                , projections = projections
                                , extraBinds = extraBinds
                                }


-- | `selectToList db sel` — run the projection and return the projected `row`
-- for every matched join row. Lowers to ONE parameterized statement
-- (`SELECT proj … FROM ta AS a0, tb AS a1 WHERE …`) through `Db.findProjection`:
-- every projected identifier is re-validated at the runtime boundary, and every
-- filter value is a bound parameter. A poisoned projection (an inherited filter
-- naming an unknown column) fails closed with the typed `Err`.
--
-- The `Select` argument must be a `Store.select` projection expression: the
-- compiler reads the projected shape and each column's type from it and emits the
-- concrete per-column decode at this call site (a single column yields the scalar,
-- a tuple yields the tuple), so no decoder is stored in the value. A `Select` that
-- is not a `Store.select` expression is a fail-closed build error (IPE-L0149); the
-- body below is that fail-closed fallback, reached only when the intercept does
-- not fire.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.join books .authorId authors .id
--         |> Store.select (\( book, author ) -> ( book.title, author.name ))
--         |> Store.selectToList db
--         -- : Task Error (List ( String, String ))
selectToList : Db -> Select row -> Task Error (List row)
selectToList conn sel =
    Task.fail selectNotProjectionError


-- | `selectToMaybe db sel` — run the projection and return the FIRST projected
-- `row`, or `Nothing`. Like `selectToList`, the `Select` argument must be a
-- `Store.select` projection expression (the decode is emitted at this call site).
selectToMaybe : Db -> Select row -> Task Error (Maybe row)
selectToMaybe conn sel =
    Task.fail selectNotProjectionError


selectNotProjectionError : Error
selectNotProjectionError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: selectToList / selectToMaybe require a `Store.select` projection expression"


-- Run the projection, decode every matched row through `decode`, and return the
-- typed list. The `selectToList` intercept emits this call with the concrete
-- per-column `decode` (`Row -> Result Error row`) built from the projection's
-- known shape, so the decode is monomorphic and stored nowhere. A row that does
-- not decode fails the whole task closed (`Result.combine` returns the first
-- `Err`, lifted to `Task.fail`). Internal to the emitted decode; not exposed.
selectDecodeList : (Row -> Result Error row) -> Db -> Select row -> Task Error (List row)
selectDecodeList decode conn sel =
    Task.andThen
        (\rows -> Task.fromResult (Result.combine (List.map decode rows)))
        (selectRows conn sel)


-- The `selectToMaybe` counterpart: decode every row, then keep the first. Same
-- fail-closed decode as `selectDecodeList`. Internal to the emitted decode.
selectDecodeMaybe : (Row -> Result Error row) -> Db -> Select row -> Task Error (Maybe row)
selectDecodeMaybe decode conn sel =
    Task.map List.head (selectDecodeList decode conn sel)


-- Run the projection and return each matched join row as a `Row` keyed by the
-- projection output names (`p0`, …). The untyped read `selectDecodeList` wraps
-- with the concrete per-column decode; not part of the caller surface.
selectRows : Db -> Select row -> Task Error (List Row)
selectRows conn sel =
    case sel of
        Select r ->
            case r.poison of
                Just e ->
                    Task.fail e

                Nothing ->
                    case r.order of
                        Nothing ->
                            Db.findProjection conn
                                r.leftTable
                                r.joinedA
                                r.rightTable
                                r.joinedB
                                r.frag
                                r.projections
                                r.extraBinds

                        Just ( orderAlias, orderCol, orderAsc ) ->
                            Db.findProjectionOrdered conn
                                r.leftTable
                                r.joinedA
                                r.rightTable
                                r.joinedB
                                r.frag
                                r.projections
                                r.extraBinds
                                orderAlias
                                orderCol
                                orderAsc


-- The projection output name a projected column binds to, by position: the first
-- projected column is `p0`, the second `p1`, and so on. Both the SQL `SELECT`
-- alias (built in the runtime from this same convention) and the emitted decode
-- read a cell by this name, so the projection list and the decode cannot disagree
-- on a column's position.
projOutputName : Int -> String
projOutputName index =
    String.concat [ "p", String.fromInt index ]


-- Read the projected column bound to position `index` back as `String`. The
-- concrete decode the `selectToList` / `selectToMaybe` intercept emits calls one
-- `projectionRead*` per projected column, keyed by the same `p<index>` output
-- name the runtime projects into. Internal to the emitted decode; not exposed.
projectionReadText : Int -> Row -> Result Error String
projectionReadText index row =
    readText (projOutputName index) row


-- Read the projected column bound to position `index` back as `Int`. Internal to
-- the emitted decode; not exposed.
projectionReadInt : Int -> Row -> Result Error Int
projectionReadInt index row =
    readInt (projOutputName index) row


-- Read the projected column bound to position `index` back as `Bool`. Internal to
-- the emitted decode; not exposed.
projectionReadBool : Int -> Row -> Result Error Bool
projectionReadBool index row =
    readBool (projOutputName index) row


-- Read the projected column bound to position `index` back as `Float`. Internal
-- to the emitted decode; not exposed.
projectionReadFloat : Int -> Row -> Result Error Float
projectionReadFloat index row =
    readFloat (projOutputName index) row


-- Combine two-to-five per-column reads (`Row -> Result Error t`) into the
-- projected tuple, threading each read's `Result` (the first `Err` wins). The
-- `selectToList` / `selectToMaybe` intercept emits `projectionDecodeN` applied to
-- the concrete per-column readers for the projected tuple's shape, so the decode
-- is monomorphic and the tuple width is fixed at the call site. Internal to the
-- emitted decode; not exposed.
projectionDecode2 : (Row -> Result Error a) -> (Row -> Result Error b) -> Row -> Result Error ( a, b )
projectionDecode2 rd0 rd1 row =
    Result.map2 (\a b -> ( a, b )) (rd0 row) (rd1 row)


projectionDecode3 : (Row -> Result Error a) -> (Row -> Result Error b) -> (Row -> Result Error c) -> Row -> Result Error ( a, b, c )
projectionDecode3 rd0 rd1 rd2 row =
    Result.map3 (\a b c -> ( a, b, c )) (rd0 row) (rd1 row) (rd2 row)


projectionDecode4 : (Row -> Result Error a) -> (Row -> Result Error b) -> (Row -> Result Error c) -> (Row -> Result Error d) -> Row -> Result Error ( a, b, c, d )
projectionDecode4 rd0 rd1 rd2 rd3 row =
    Result.map4 (\a b c d -> ( a, b, c, d )) (rd0 row) (rd1 row) (rd2 row) (rd3 row)


projectionDecode5 : (Row -> Result Error a) -> (Row -> Result Error b) -> (Row -> Result Error c) -> (Row -> Result Error d) -> (Row -> Result Error e) -> Row -> Result Error ( a, b, c, d, e )
projectionDecode5 rd0 rd1 rd2 rd3 rd4 row =
    Result.map5 (\a b c d e -> ( a, b, c, d, e )) (rd0 row) (rd1 row) (rd2 row) (rd3 row) (rd4 row)


-- Decode each `(leftRow, rightRow)` cell-map pair through the two codecs into a
-- typed `( a, b )` pair. A pair whose either side does not decode to its store's
-- type is a typed `Err`, never a wrong value.
decodeJoinRows : Codec a -> Codec b -> List ( Row, Row ) -> Task Error (List ( a, b ))
decodeJoinRows codecA codecB pairs =
    case pairs of
        [] ->
            Task.succeed []

        first :: rest ->
            case first of
                ( leftRow, rightRow ) ->
                    case DbCodec.codecFromRow codecA leftRow of
                        Err e ->
                            Task.fail e

                        Ok a ->
                            case DbCodec.codecFromRow codecB rightRow of
                                Err e ->
                                    Task.fail e

                                Ok b ->
                                    Task.map
                                        (\more -> ( a, b ) :: more)
                                        (decodeJoinRows codecA codecB rest)


-- ── Cond → SqlFragment lowering (the single validation + emit point) ──


-- Lower a `Cond` to an audited `SqlFragment`, validating every column against
-- the store's own column list first. A column absent from that list is a typed
-- `Err` (parse-don't-validate); an accepted column reaches SQL only through
-- `Sql.column`, and every value only through `Sql.param`. `where` calls this the
-- moment a `Cond` is added (the store is in hand), so a bad column fails closed
-- at build time and no `Cond` value survives past the query builder.
condFragmentIn : List Column -> Cond row -> Result Error SqlFragment
condFragmentIn columns cond =
    case cond of
        Compare op col value ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (compareFragment op col value)

        Like col pattern ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.like (Sql.column col) pattern)

        IsNull col ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.isNull (Sql.column col))

        NotNull col ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.isNotNull (Sql.column col))

        InList col values ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.inList (Sql.column col) values)

        AndList conds ->
            foldConds columns Sql.and trueFragment conds

        OrList conds ->
            foldConds columns Sql.or falseFragment conds

        NotCond inner ->
            Result.map Sql.not (condFragmentIn columns inner)


-- Validate `col` against the store's columns, returning `built` when accepted or
-- a fail-closed `Err` when the store names no such column.
checkColumn : List Column -> String -> SqlFragment -> Result Error SqlFragment
checkColumn columns col built =
    if hasColumn columns col then
        Ok built

    else
        Err (unknownColumnError col)


compareFragment : CompareOp -> String -> SqlValue -> SqlFragment
compareFragment op col value =
    let
        lhs =
            Sql.column col

        rhs =
            Sql.param value
    in
    case op of
        OpEq ->
            Sql.eq lhs rhs

        OpNeq ->
            Sql.ne lhs rhs

        OpGt ->
            Sql.gt lhs rhs

        OpGte ->
            Sql.gte lhs rhs

        OpLt ->
            Sql.lt lhs rhs

        OpLte ->
            Sql.lte lhs rhs


-- Lower a `Cond` for one join side to an audited `SqlFragment`, validating every
-- column against that side's own column list first and QUALIFYING each accepted
-- column with the side's table alias (`col` → `alias.col`) before it reaches
-- SQL. The bare column is what the store's derived list names, so the
-- membership check stays against `columns`; only the emitted reference is
-- qualified, so the join's WHERE names each side unambiguously. A column absent
-- from the side's store is a typed `Err` (parse-don't-validate); every value
-- binds through `Sql.param`. This is `condFragmentIn` with the alias qualifier —
-- the two share the same validation contract, differing only in the emitted
-- column reference.
condFragmentInQualified : String -> List Column -> Cond row -> Result Error SqlFragment
condFragmentInQualified alias columns cond =
    case cond of
        Compare op col value ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (compareFragment op (qualified alias col) value)

        Like col pattern ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.like (Sql.column (qualified alias col)) pattern)

        IsNull col ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.isNull (Sql.column (qualified alias col)))

        NotNull col ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.isNotNull (Sql.column (qualified alias col)))

        InList col values ->
            checkColumn columns col
                (Sql.inList (Sql.column (qualified alias col)) values)

        AndList conds ->
            foldCondsQualified alias columns Sql.and trueFragment conds

        OrList conds ->
            foldCondsQualified alias columns Sql.or falseFragment conds

        NotCond inner ->
            Result.map Sql.not (condFragmentInQualified alias columns inner)


-- The alias-qualifying counterpart to `foldConds`, threading the side's alias
-- through each recursive lowering.
foldCondsQualified : String -> List Column -> (SqlFragment -> SqlFragment -> SqlFragment) -> SqlFragment -> List (Cond row) -> Result Error SqlFragment
foldCondsQualified alias columns join emptyFragment conds =
    case conds of
        [] ->
            Ok emptyFragment

        [ single ] ->
            condFragmentInQualified alias columns single

        first :: rest ->
            case condFragmentInQualified alias columns first of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok head ->
                    Result.map (\tail -> join head tail)
                        (foldCondsQualified alias columns join emptyFragment rest)


-- Fold a list of conditions with a binary connective, using `emptyFragment` as
-- the identity for the empty list. Each condition is lowered (and column-checked)
-- before it joins, so the first invalid column fails the whole fold closed.
foldConds : List Column -> (SqlFragment -> SqlFragment -> SqlFragment) -> SqlFragment -> List (Cond row) -> Result Error SqlFragment
foldConds columns join emptyFragment conds =
    case conds of
        [] ->
            Ok emptyFragment

        [ single ] ->
            condFragmentIn columns single

        first :: rest ->
            case condFragmentIn columns first of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok head ->
                    Result.map (\tail -> join head tail)
                        (foldConds columns join emptyFragment rest)


-- The always-true / always-false fragments, the identities of `and` / `or` over
-- the empty list. Each names no caller identifier, so neither adds any injection
-- surface.
trueFragment : SqlFragment
trueFragment =
    Sql.eq (Sql.param (SqlInt 1)) (Sql.param (SqlInt 1))


falseFragment : SqlFragment
falseFragment =
    Sql.eq (Sql.param (SqlInt 1)) (Sql.param (SqlInt 0))


-- ── Ordering + pagination over raw rows (in Ipê, no SQL text) ──────


-- Order then paginate the raw rows: sort by each ordering key (numeric-aware for
-- `CInt` / `CReal` columns), skip `off` rows, keep `lim` rows.
paginateRows : List OrderKey -> Maybe Int -> Maybe Int -> List Row -> List Row
paginateRows orderings off lim rows =
    applyLimit lim (applyOffset off (orderRowsWith orderings rows))


-- Sort raw rows by the ordering keys, in priority order: the LAST ordering is
-- applied first so a stable sort leaves the FIRST ordering as the dominant key
-- (the primary sort). A raw `Row` is a `Dict String String`, so the sort
-- compares the string cell, numerically when the column type is `CInt` / `CReal`.
orderRowsWith : List OrderKey -> List Row -> List Row
orderRowsWith orderings rows =
    List.foldr
        (\ordering acc -> sortByOrdering ordering acc)
        rows
        orderings


sortByOrdering : OrderKey -> List Row -> List Row
sortByOrdering ordering rows =
    case ordering of
        ( col, numeric, ascending ) ->
            List.sortWith (\a b -> cellCompare numeric ascending col a b) rows


cellCompare : Bool -> Bool -> String -> Row -> Row -> Order
cellCompare numeric ascending col a b =
    let
        base =
            compareCells numeric (rowCell col a) (rowCell col b)
    in
    if ascending then
        base

    else
        flipOrder base


compareCells : Bool -> String -> String -> Order
compareCells numeric x y =
    if numeric then
        compareNumeric x y

    else
        compare x y


compareNumeric : String -> String -> Order
compareNumeric x y =
    case ( String.toFloat x, String.toFloat y ) of
        ( Just fx, Just fy ) ->
            compare fx fy

        _ ->
            compare x y


isNumericType : ColType -> Bool
isNumericType colType =
    case colType of
        CInt ->
            True

        CReal ->
            True

        CNull inner ->
            isNumericType inner

        CText ->
            False

        CBool ->
            False

        CBlob ->
            False


flipOrder : Order -> Order
flipOrder ord =
    case ord of
        LT ->
            GT

        EQ ->
            EQ

        GT ->
            LT


applyOffset : Maybe Int -> List Row -> List Row
applyOffset off rows =
    case off of
        Just n ->
            List.drop n rows

        Nothing ->
            rows


applyLimit : Maybe Int -> List Row -> List Row
applyLimit lim rows =
    case lim of
        Just n ->
            List.take n rows

        Nothing ->
            rows


-- ─── Typed column reads (row -> value) ─────────────────────────────
--
-- `Db.findWhere` returns each row as `Dict String String`. These helpers read
-- one column back into its Ipê type, failing closed with a typed error when the
-- column is absent or malformed — never a silent default. They are the readers
-- for a raw-column `Store Row`: a codec-driven store decodes whole rows through
-- its own codec, but a raw store reads columns individually.


-- | Read a column as a `String`. A missing column is a typed `Err`.
readText : String -> Row -> Result Error String
readText name row =
    case Dict.get name row of
        Just s ->
            Ok s

        Nothing ->
            Err (missingColumnError name)


-- | Read a column as an `Int`. A missing or non-integer column is a typed `Err`.
readInt : String -> Row -> Result Error Int
readInt name row =
    case Dict.get name row of
        Just s ->
            case String.toInt s of
                Just n ->
                    Ok n

                Nothing ->
                    Err (badColumnError name "Int" s)

        Nothing ->
            Err (missingColumnError name)


-- | Read a column as a `Float`. A missing or non-numeric column is a typed
-- `Err`.
readFloat : String -> Row -> Result Error Float
readFloat name row =
    case Dict.get name row of
        Just s ->
            case String.toFloat s of
                Just f ->
                    Ok f

                Nothing ->
                    Err (badColumnError name "Float" s)

        Nothing ->
            Err (missingColumnError name)


-- | Read a column as a `Bool`. The stored form is SQLite's `0` / `1` integer
-- convention (and the literals `true` / `false`); anything else is a typed
-- `Err`.
readBool : String -> Row -> Result Error Bool
readBool name row =
    case Dict.get name row of
        Just s ->
            if s == "1" || s == "true" then
                Ok True

            else if s == "0" || s == "false" then
                Ok False

            else
                Err (badColumnError name "Bool" s)

        Nothing ->
            Err (missingColumnError name)


-- ─── Schema (create / migrations) ──────────────────────────────────


-- | `createSql store` — the `CREATE TABLE` DDL for the store, or `Err`. Every
-- identifier that would appear in the DDL — the table name and each column name
-- — is re-validated through `validSqlIdent` before it can reach the string, so
-- a store built by a constructor (whose identifiers already validate) always
-- yields `Ok`, and any identifier that somehow does not validate fails closed
-- with no DDL produced. This is the one place a Store builds SQL text for the
-- create entry; it builds only from validated identifiers and holds no values,
-- so there is nothing to inject.
createSql : Store a -> Result Error String
createSql store =
    case store of
        Store r ->
            createSqlFromColumns r.table r.frozenColumns r.specs


createSqlFromColumns : String -> List Column -> List ColumnSpec -> Result Error String
createSqlFromColumns table cols specs =
    if Basics.not (validSqlIdent table) then
        Err (invalidIdentError "table" table)

    else
        case specsWithinColumns specs cols of
            Err e ->
                Err e

            Ok _ ->
                case columnDefs specs cols of
                    Err e ->
                        Err e

                    Ok defs ->
                        Ok
                            (String.concat
                                [ "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS "
                                , table
                                , " ("
                                , String.join ", " defs
                                , ")"
                                ]
                            )


-- Every spec must name one of the codec's own derived columns; a spec for a
-- column absent from the codec is a silent no-op otherwise (its constraint
-- never attaches to any emitted column), so fail closed — mirroring how the
-- query leaves validate a `Cond` column with `condFragmentIn`. Defence in
-- depth: the accessor builders and `Codec.auto` both snake_case through the
-- one transform, so the names should already agree; this rejects the case
-- where they somehow do not, instead of silently dropping the constraint.
specsWithinColumns : List ColumnSpec -> List Column -> Result Error ()
specsWithinColumns specs cols =
    case specs of
        [] ->
            Ok ()

        first :: rest ->
            let
                name =
                    specColumn first
            in
            if hasColumn cols name then
                specsWithinColumns rest cols

            else
                Err (unknownColumnError name)


-- The column a spec constrains — every `ColumnSpec` variant carries it as its
-- first field.
specColumn : ColumnSpec -> String
specColumn spec =
    case spec of
        PrimaryKey c ->
            c

        Serial c ->
            c

        Unique c ->
            c

        DefaultNow c ->
            c

        DefaultText c _ ->
            c

        DefaultInt c _ ->
            c

        TouchOnUpdate c ->
            c


columnDefs : List ColumnSpec -> List Column -> Result Error (List String)
columnDefs specs columns =
    case columns of
        [] ->
            Ok []

        first :: rest ->
            case columnDef specs first of
                Err e ->
                    Err e

                Ok def ->
                    Result.map (\more -> def :: more) (columnDefs specs rest)


columnDef : List ColumnSpec -> Column -> Result Error String
columnDef specs col =
    let
        name =
            columnName col
    in
    if Basics.not (validSqlIdent name) then
        Err (invalidIdentError "column" name)

    else if List.any (\s -> specIsSerial s name) specs && Basics.not (List.any (\s -> specIsPrimaryKey s name) specs) then
        Err (serialWithoutPkError name)

    else
        Ok
            (String.concat
                [ name
                , " "
                , sqlType (columnColType col)
                , columnConstraints specs name
                ]
            )


sqlType : ColType -> String
sqlType colType =
    case colType of
        CText ->
            "TEXT"

        CInt ->
            "INTEGER"

        CReal ->
            "REAL"

        CBool ->
            "INTEGER"

        CBlob ->
            "TEXT"

        CNull inner ->
            sqlType inner


-- | Build the SQL constraint suffix for `name` from the store's specs.
--
-- SQLite requires `PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT` in exactly that order; emitting
-- `AUTOINCREMENT` before `PRIMARY KEY` is a syntax error (SQLITE_ERROR).
-- Instead of concatenating each spec's fragment in arbitrary list order, we
-- emit in the canonical SQL constraint order:
--   PRIMARY KEY  →  AUTOINCREMENT  →  UNIQUE  →  DEFAULT …
-- A column that is both `Serial` and `PrimaryKey` therefore always gets
-- `PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT`, regardless of the order the caller applied
-- those specs.
columnConstraints : List ColumnSpec -> String -> String
columnConstraints specs name =
    let
        isPk =
            List.any (\s -> specIsPrimaryKey s name) specs

        isSerial =
            List.any (\s -> specIsSerial s name) specs

        isUnique =
            List.any (\s -> specIsUnique s name) specs

        defaultNowFragment =
            if List.any (\s -> specIsDefaultNow s name) specs then
                " DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP"

            else
                ""

        defaultLiteralFragment =
            case List.filterMap (\s -> specDefaultLiteral s name) specs of
                literal :: _ ->
                    String.concat [ " DEFAULT ", literal ]

                [] ->
                    ""

        pkFragment =
            if isPk then
                " PRIMARY KEY"

            else
                ""

        autoincFragment =
            if isSerial then
                " AUTOINCREMENT"

            else
                ""

        uniqueFragment =
            if isUnique && Basics.not isPk then
                " UNIQUE"

            else
                ""
    in
    String.concat [ pkFragment, autoincFragment, uniqueFragment, defaultNowFragment, defaultLiteralFragment ]


specIsPrimaryKey : ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
specIsPrimaryKey spec name =
    case spec of
        PrimaryKey c ->
            c == name

        Serial _ ->
            False

        Unique _ ->
            False

        DefaultNow _ ->
            False

        DefaultText _ _ ->
            False

        DefaultInt _ _ ->
            False

        TouchOnUpdate _ ->
            False


specIsSerial : ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
specIsSerial spec name =
    case spec of
        Serial c ->
            c == name

        PrimaryKey _ ->
            False

        Unique _ ->
            False

        DefaultNow _ ->
            False

        DefaultText _ _ ->
            False

        DefaultInt _ _ ->
            False

        TouchOnUpdate _ ->
            False


specIsUnique : ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
specIsUnique spec name =
    case spec of
        Unique c ->
            c == name

        PrimaryKey _ ->
            False

        Serial _ ->
            False

        DefaultNow _ ->
            False

        DefaultText _ _ ->
            False

        DefaultInt _ _ ->
            False

        TouchOnUpdate _ ->
            False


specIsDefaultNow : ColumnSpec -> String -> Bool
specIsDefaultNow spec name =
    case spec of
        DefaultNow c ->
            c == name

        TouchOnUpdate c ->
            c == name

        PrimaryKey _ ->
            False

        Serial _ ->
            False

        Unique _ ->
            False

        DefaultText _ _ ->
            False

        DefaultInt _ _ ->
            False


-- The literal `DEFAULT` clause a typed default spec contributes for `name`, or
-- `Nothing` when `spec` sets no literal default there. `DefaultInt` renders as
-- its own digits (no injection surface); `DefaultText` is emitted as an escaped
-- single-quoted SQL string literal (every embedded quote doubled), so no caller
-- text can break out of the literal.
specDefaultLiteral : ColumnSpec -> String -> Maybe String
specDefaultLiteral spec name =
    case spec of
        DefaultText c value ->
            if c == name then
                Just (sqlTextLiteral value)

            else
                Nothing

        DefaultInt c value ->
            if c == name then
                Just (String.fromInt value)

            else
                Nothing

        PrimaryKey _ ->
            Nothing

        Serial _ ->
            Nothing

        Unique _ ->
            Nothing

        DefaultNow _ ->
            Nothing

        TouchOnUpdate _ ->
            Nothing


-- Render `value` as a single-quoted SQL string literal, doubling every embedded
-- single quote. This is the standard SQL literal escape: a `value` containing a
-- quote becomes `''` inside the literal, so it can never terminate the literal
-- and start new SQL. Used only for the DDL `DEFAULT` clause, where a bound
-- parameter is not permitted.
sqlTextLiteral : String -> String
sqlTextLiteral value =
    String.concat [ "'", String.replace "'" "''" value, "'" ]


-- | `create conn store` — create the store's table if it does not exist, via
-- the versioned `Db.migrate` ledger. For a store with rename ops, use
-- `migrations` to obtain the full ordered entry list instead. Fails closed if
-- the DDL cannot be built from validated identifiers (no SQL is issued in that
-- case). Idempotent: the ledger applies the migration once and records it.
create : Db -> Store a -> Task Error (List String)
create conn store =
    case migrations store of
        Err e ->
            Task.fail e

        Ok ms ->
            Db.migrate conn ms


migrationName : String -> String
migrationName table =
    String.concat [ "create_", table ]


-- ─── Row-level security (Policy algebra + Secured constructor) ──────
--
-- Row security is DATA, not closures: a `Policy` is a small algebra over the
-- store's own validated column names, never a stored function (Ipê forbids
-- functions in records). A policy is a conjunction of `Rule`s — an owner-column
-- rule (rows are scoped to the principal that owns the accessor-named column), a
-- public-read rule, or an immutable-column rule. Each column-naming rule names
-- its column by an accessor literal (`.author`), checked against the row type at
-- compile time exactly like a query column, so a policy over a non-existent or
-- cross-row column is a build error.
--
-- Deny-by-default: a `Draft` is unqueryable until classified. A `Secured` store
-- is reachable only by attaching a policy through `secured`, and the
-- authenticated `…As` operations accept only a `Secured`. `secured` re-validates
-- every policy column against the draft's codec-derived columns (the same
-- fail-closed check the query leaves use), so a policy that names a column the
-- draft does not have is a typed `Err`, never a silently ineffective policy.


-- | One row-security rule over a validated column name. Internal to this module:
-- a `Policy` is opaque, so a rule is never constructed or inspected by a caller —
-- it is built only by the accessor-typed policy builders and consumed only by the
-- module's own `secured` re-validation (and, in a later slice, the fragment
-- lowering). `PublicRead` names no column; the column-naming rules carry the
-- accessor-derived, snake_cased column name.
type Rule
    = OwnerColumn String
    | PublicRead
    | Immutable String


-- | An opaque row-security policy: a conjunction of `Rule`s over a store's
-- validated columns. Phantom in `row` so an accessor-named policy column is
-- checked against the store's row type when the policy is attached with
-- `secured` (a cross-row policy column is a type error). The runtime value
-- carries only the rule list — the `row` parameter is erased, exactly as the
-- `Cond row` phantom is.
type Policy row
    = Policy (List Rule)


-- | An opaque secured store: a `Store a` paired with a validated `Policy`. The
-- ONLY value the future secured operations (`allAs` / `insertAs` / …) will
-- accept, so a secured store is unreachable through the unsecured operations and
-- cannot be read unfiltered by mistake (deny-by-default). Built only by
-- `secured`, which re-validates every policy column against the store's columns,
-- so a `Secured` holds only a policy whose columns all exist on the store.
--
-- Parametric in the row type `a` (it carries the store's `Codec a`) so the later
-- secured reads can decode rows through the store's own codec — the appendix
-- sketch wrote a bare `Secured`, but erasing `a` would lose the codec the
-- secured reads need. A reasoned divergence recorded here.
type Secured a
    = Secured (Store a) (Policy a)


-- | `unrestricted` — the policy that allows all rows (an explicit empty
-- conjunction). A store secured with `unrestricted` filters nothing; it exists so
-- "no restriction" is a deliberate, named choice rather than the absence of a
-- policy (which, deny-by-default, is instead an unsecured store).
unrestricted : Policy row
unrestricted =
    Policy []


-- | `publicRead` — a policy of one public-read rule: reads are unrestricted while
-- writes still route through the secured operations. A plain value (it names no
-- column, so it needs no accessor).
publicRead : Policy row
publicRead =
    Policy [ PublicRead ]


-- | `ownerColumn accessor` — a policy of one owner-column rule over the
-- accessor-named column: rows are scoped to the principal that owns that column.
-- The column is named by an accessor literal (`.author`), checked against the
-- row type at compile time and snake_cased to the validated column name at
-- lowering (the same derivation `Codec.auto` uses), then delegated to
-- `ownerColumnNamed`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.ownerColumn .author
ownerColumn : (row -> t) -> Policy row
ownerColumn =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_ownerColumn"


-- | `immutable accessor` — a policy of one immutable-column rule over the
-- accessor-named column: the column cannot be changed after insert. Named by an
-- accessor literal, checked against the row type, delegated to `immutableNamed`.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.immutable .createdAt
immutable : (row -> t) -> Policy row
immutable =
    Ffi.kernel "Store_immutable"


-- The stringly forms reached by the accessor-typed policy-builder intercepts
-- above. The accessor `.field` has already been turned into the validated,
-- snake_cased column name `col`, so each builds a single-rule policy. Not part of
-- the caller surface for column naming (a caller writes the accessor); exported
-- only so the intercept can name them.
ownerColumnNamed : String -> Policy row
ownerColumnNamed col =
    Policy [ OwnerColumn col ]


immutableNamed : String -> Policy row
immutableNamed col =
    Policy [ Immutable col ]


-- | `andPolicy a b` — the conjunction of two policies (their rules concatenated).
-- Named `andPolicy` rather than `and` because `Store.and` is already the `Cond`
-- connective (`List (Cond row) -> Cond row`); Ipê has no ad-hoc overloading, so
-- the policy conjunction takes a distinct name.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.ownerColumn .author
--         |> Store.andPolicy (Store.immutable .createdAt)
andPolicy : Policy row -> Policy row -> Policy row
andPolicy extra base =
    case base of
        Policy baseRules ->
            case extra of
                Policy extraRules ->
                    Policy (baseRules ++ extraRules)


-- | `secured policy draft` — classify `draft` by attaching `policy`, or fail
-- closed. This is one of the two `Draft` → queryable promotions (`public` is the
-- other): it yields an opaque `Secured a`, the only value the authenticated
-- `…As` operations accept. Every column the policy names is re-validated against
-- the draft's own codec-derived columns (the same `hasColumn` check the query
-- leaves and `specsWithinColumns` use): a policy column absent from the draft is
-- a typed `Err`, never a silently ineffective policy. Deny-by-default holds — a
-- table is queryable only through a deliberate classification, and a `secured`
-- table is reachable only with a `Principal` through the `…As` operations.
--
-- Example:
--
--     Store.secured
--         (Store.ownerColumn .author
--             |> Store.andPolicy (Store.immutable .createdAt)
--         )
--         draft
secured : Policy a -> Draft a -> Result Error (Secured a)
secured policy draft =
    case draft of
        Draft r ->
            case policy of
                Policy rules ->
                    case firstUnknownPolicyColumn r.currentColumns rules of
                        Just bad ->
                            Err (unknownColumnError bad)

                        Nothing ->
                            Ok (Secured (public draft) policy)


-- The first column named by a rule that is not one of the store's columns, or
-- `Nothing` when every column-naming rule names a real store column. `PublicRead`
-- names no column, so it is always in-policy.
firstUnknownPolicyColumn : List Column -> List Rule -> Maybe String
firstUnknownPolicyColumn columns rules =
    case rules of
        [] ->
            Nothing

        first :: rest ->
            case ruleColumn first of
                Just name ->
                    if hasColumn columns name then
                        firstUnknownPolicyColumn columns rest

                    else
                        Just name

                Nothing ->
                    firstUnknownPolicyColumn columns rest


-- The column a rule constrains, or `Nothing` for a rule that names no column
-- (`PublicRead`).
ruleColumn : Rule -> Maybe String
ruleColumn rule =
    case rule of
        OwnerColumn c ->
            Just c

        Immutable c ->
            Just c

        PublicRead ->
            Nothing


-- ─── Secured operations (the ONLY ops that accept a `Secured`) ──────
--
-- Each `…As` op takes the authenticated `Principal` and a `Secured` store, and
-- ANDs the policy's owner filter into every read/write. The filter binds the
-- principal's subject as a SQL PARAMETER through `Sql.param` — never string
-- interpolation — so no caller identity can inject SQL. A `Secured` is reachable
-- only through `secured` (which re-validates the policy columns), and the
-- unsecured operations do not accept a `Secured`, so a secured store is
-- unreadable and unwritable without a principal and its policy (deny-by-default).


-- | `policyFragment principal policy` — the `WHERE` fragment a policy compiles
-- to for `principal`: every `OwnerColumn` rule becomes `owner = $subject` (the
-- validated column through `Sql.column`, the subject BOUND through `Sql.param`),
-- all `AND`-ed together. `PublicRead` and `Immutable` add no read filter, so a
-- policy of only those matches every row (the read is unrestricted while the
-- write path stays secured). The empty conjunction is `alwaysTrue`.
policyFragment : Auth.Principal -> Policy a -> SqlFragment
policyFragment principal policy =
    case policy of
        Policy rules ->
            List.foldl
                (\rule acc -> Sql.and acc (ruleFragment principal rule))
                alwaysTrue
                rules


-- The fragment a single rule contributes for `principal`. An `OwnerColumn` binds
-- the subject as a parameter against its validated column; `Immutable` and
-- `PublicRead` add no read constraint (`alwaysTrue`).
ruleFragment : Auth.Principal -> Rule -> SqlFragment
ruleFragment principal rule =
    case rule of
        OwnerColumn col ->
            Sql.eq (Sql.column col) (Sql.param (SqlString (Auth.subject principal)))

        Immutable _ ->
            alwaysTrue

        PublicRead ->
            alwaysTrue


-- | `allAs principal db secured` — read every row the policy admits for
-- `principal`, decoded through the store's codec. The policy's owner filter is
-- the WHERE, so the database never returns an out-of-policy row.
allAs : Auth.Principal -> Db -> Secured a -> Task Error (List a)
allAs principal conn secured =
    case secured of
        Secured store policy ->
            case store of
                Store r ->
                    Task.andThen
                        (\rows -> decodeRows r.codec rows)
                        (Db.findWhere conn r.table (policyFragment principal policy))


-- | `getAs principal db secured keyValue` — read the single row whose primary
-- key equals `keyValue` AND which the policy admits for `principal`, or
-- `Nothing`. Keys on the store's validated `pk`; both the key and the subject
-- bind as parameters. A store with no primary key fails closed.
getAs : Auth.Principal -> Db -> Secured a -> SqlValue -> Task Error (Maybe a)
getAs principal conn secured keyValue =
    case secured of
        Secured store policy ->
            case store of
                Store r ->
                    case r.pk of
                        Nothing ->
                            Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                        Just keyCol ->
                            Task.andThen
                                (\rows -> firstDecoded r.codec rows)
                                (Db.findWhere conn
                                    r.table
                                    (Sql.and
                                        (keyEquals keyCol keyValue)
                                        (policyFragment principal policy)
                                    )
                                )


-- | `insertAs principal db secured row` — insert `row`, forcing every
-- `OwnerColumn` to `principal`'s subject so a caller can never write a row it
-- would not be allowed to read back. The owner value binds as a parameter. The
-- inserted-row count is returned.
insertAs : Auth.Principal -> Db -> Secured a -> a -> Task Error Int
insertAs principal conn secured row =
    case secured of
        Secured store policy ->
            case store of
                Store r ->
                    case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                        Err e ->
                            Task.fail e

                        Ok binds ->
                            Db.insertFields conn
                                r.table
                                (insertFields r.specs
                                    (forceOwnerColumns principal policy binds)
                                )


-- | `updateAs principal db secured row` — update the row whose primary key
-- matches `row`, but only when the policy admits it for `principal`; the owner
-- filter is AND-ed into the WHERE so a caller cannot update another principal's
-- row. Columns marked `Serial` / `DefaultNow` are omitted from the SET. A store
-- with no primary key, or a record missing its key column, fails closed.
updateAs : Auth.Principal -> Db -> Secured a -> a -> Task Error Int
updateAs principal conn secured row =
    case secured of
        Secured store policy ->
            case store of
                Store r ->
                    case r.pk of
                        Nothing ->
                            Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                        Just keyCol ->
                            case DbCodec.codecToBinds r.codec row of
                                Err e ->
                                    Task.fail e

                                Ok binds ->
                                    case bindValue keyCol binds of
                                        Nothing ->
                                            Task.fail (missingKeyBindError keyCol)

                                        Just keyValue ->
                                            Db.updateWhere conn
                                                r.table
                                                (updateSetFields r.specs keyCol binds)
                                                (Sql.and
                                                    (keyEquals keyCol keyValue)
                                                    (policyFragment principal policy)
                                                )


-- | `deleteAs principal db secured keyValue` — delete the row whose primary key
-- equals `keyValue`, but only when the policy admits it for `principal`; the
-- owner filter is AND-ed into the WHERE. Keys on the validated `pk`; both values
-- bind as parameters. A store with no primary key fails closed.
deleteAs : Auth.Principal -> Db -> Secured a -> SqlValue -> Task Error Int
deleteAs principal conn secured keyValue =
    case secured of
        Secured store policy ->
            case store of
                Store r ->
                    case r.pk of
                        Nothing ->
                            Task.fail noPrimaryKeyError

                        Just keyCol ->
                            Db.deleteWhere conn
                                r.table
                                (Sql.and
                                    (keyEquals keyCol keyValue)
                                    (policyFragment principal policy)
                                )


-- Force every `OwnerColumn` named by the policy to `principal`'s subject in a
-- codec projection, so an insert cannot set an owner other than the caller. A
-- rule that names no column (`PublicRead`) and `Immutable` leave the binds
-- unchanged.
forceOwnerColumns : Auth.Principal -> Policy a -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlValue )
forceOwnerColumns principal policy binds =
    case policy of
        Policy rules ->
            List.foldl (\rule acc -> forceOwnerColumn principal rule acc) binds rules


forceOwnerColumn : Auth.Principal -> Rule -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlValue )
forceOwnerColumn principal rule binds =
    case rule of
        OwnerColumn col ->
            setBind col (SqlString (Auth.subject principal)) binds

        Immutable _ ->
            binds

        PublicRead ->
            binds


-- Set `col`'s value to `value` in a codec projection: replace the existing pair
-- when present, or append it when the column is absent from the projection.
setBind : String -> SqlValue -> List ( String, SqlValue ) -> List ( String, SqlValue )
setBind col value binds =
    if List.any (\pair -> bindNameIs col pair) binds then
        List.map
            (\pair ->
                if bindNameIs col pair then
                    ( col, value )

                else
                    pair
            )
            binds

    else
        binds ++ [ ( col, value ) ]


-- ─── Errors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


invalidIdentError : String -> String -> Error
invalidIdentError kind name =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat
            [ "Ipe.Db.Store: rejected ", kind, " identifier \"", name, "\" — not a valid SQL identifier" ]
        )


notARecordError : Error
notARecordError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: the codec's shape is not a record, so it declares no columns to build a store from"


noPrimaryKeyError : Error
noPrimaryKeyError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: this operation requires a primary key; declare one with Store.primaryKey"


unknownColumnError : String -> Error
unknownColumnError name =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat
            [ "Ipe.Db.Store: \"", name, "\" is not a column of this store — a query may only reference the store's own derived columns" ]
        )


unconstrainedMutationError : Error
unconstrainedMutationError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: refusing an unconstrained updateWhere/deleteWhere — the condition constrains no column (e.g. `and []` matches every row), which would mass-update or mass-delete the whole table; pass a condition that references at least one column"


missingKeyBindError : String -> Error
missingKeyBindError keyCol =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat
            [ "Ipe.Db.Store: update needs the primary-key column \"", keyCol, "\" in the record's codec projection, but it is absent" ]
        )


serialWithoutPkError : String -> Error
serialWithoutPkError name =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat
            [ "Ipe.Db.Store: column \""
            , name
            , "\" is marked serial but has no primary key — SQLite requires AUTOINCREMENT on a PRIMARY KEY column; add Store.primaryKey \""
            , name
            , "\" to the store"
            ]
        )


missingColumnError : String -> Error
missingColumnError name =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat [ "Ipe.Db.Store: row is missing column \"", name, "\"" ])


badColumnError : String -> String -> String -> Error
badColumnError name expected got =
    Error.invalidInput
        (String.concat
            [ "Ipe.Db.Store: column \"", name, "\" is not a valid ", expected, ": \"", got, "\"" ]
        )


-- | `noopRenameError` — the error returned by `migrations` when a rename op has
-- the same `from` and `to` identifier. A no-op rename would seed a redundant
-- ledger entry whose stable name (`rename_column_T_X_to_X`) is misleading and
-- whose SQL (`ALTER … RENAME COLUMN X TO X`) is a likely mistake.
noopRenameError : Error
noopRenameError =
    Error.invalidInput
        "Ipe.Db.Store: rename op has identical from and to — a no-op rename would seed a redundant ledger entry"
